_l(theta_i),\r\n        B      = sum_i w_i J_l(psi_i)^{-1},  psi_i = Log(R_m^T R_i).\r\n\r\n    The spatial blocks satisfy ``sum_i Ghat_i = I``, which makes the mean\r\n    left-equivariant: for a common superposed rotation ``Q``,\r\n    ``mean(Q R_i, w) = Q mean(R_i, w)``, and the parameter tangents satisfy\r\n    the differential rigid-spin identity\r\n    ``sum_i G_i J_l(theta_i)^{-1} = J_l(theta_m)^{-1}``, the covector\r\n    counterpart of physical wrench preservation.\r\n\r\n    Two-master means use the unique shortest-geodesic closed form.  Larger\r\n    means are restricted to an open pi/4 ball about the first master, with\r\n    the returned mean inside that same ball.  Multi-master input/output\r\n    rotation vectors must remain inside the supported principal chart.\r\n    Nonnegative partition weights are required; their common scale cancels\r\n    from the mean equation and its differential.  The direct route keeps\r\n    its existing rotation coordinates.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    thetas = [np.asarray(t, dtype=float).reshape(3).copy() for t in master_thetas]\r\n    ws = [float(w) for w in weights]\r\n    if not thetas or len(thetas) != len(ws):\r\n        raise ValueError(f\"{label}: SO(3) mean requires at least one master\")\r\n    if not all(np.all(np.isfinite(t)) for t in thetas) or not np.all(np.isfinite(ws)):\r\n        raise ValueError(f\"{label}: SO(3) mean requires finite rotations and weights\")\r\n    if any(w < 0.0 for w in ws):\r\n        raise ValueError(\r\n            f\"{label}: the intrinsic SO(3) mean on the active rotating-offset \"\r\n            f\"route requires nonnegative weights; got {ws}\"\r\n        )\r\n    weight_sum = float(sum(ws))\r\n    if abs(weight_sum - 1.0) > 1.0e-8:\r\n        raise ValueError(\r\n            f\"{label}: the intrinsic SO(3) mean requires weights forming a \"\r\n            f\"partition of unity; got sum {weight_sum!r}\"\r\n        )\r\n    if len(thetas) == 1:\r\n        return thetas[0], (np.eye(3),)\r\n    ws = [w / weight_sum for w in ws]\r\n    chart_limit = np.pi - 1.0e-6\r\n    if any(float(np.linalg.norm(t)) >= chart_limit for t in thetas):\r\n        raise ValueError(f\"{label}: interpolated SO(3) mean is outside the principal rotation-vector chart\")\r\n    first = thetas[0]\r\n    if all(np.array_equal(t, first) for t in thetas[1:]):\r\n        return first.copy(), tuple(w * np.eye(3) for w in ws)\r\n\r\n    rotations = [rotation_matrix_from_vector(t) for t in thetas]\r\n    anchor = rotations[0]\r\n    if len(thetas) == 2:\r\n        relative = rotation_vector_from_matrix(anchor.T @ rotations[1])\r\n        if float(np.linalg.norm(relative)) >= chart_limit:\r\n            raise ValueError(f\"{label}: ambiguous shortest-geodesic SO(3) mean\")\r\n        fraction = ws[1]\r\n        R_m = anchor @ rotation_matrix_from_vector(fraction * relative)\r\n        psis = [-fraction * relative, (1.0 - fraction) * relative]\r\n    else:\r\n        radius = np.pi / 4.0\r\n        if any(float(np.linalg.norm(rotation_vector_from_matrix(anchor.T @ r))) >= radius\r\n               for r in rotations):\r\n            raise ValueError(f\"{label}: SO(3) mean requires a unique convex anchor ball of radius pi/4\")\r\n        R_m = anchor.copy()\r\n        for _ in range(100):\r\n            psis = [rotation_vector_from_matrix(R_m.T @ r) for r in rotations]\r\n            f = sum((w * psi for w, psi in zip(ws, psis)), np.zeros(3))\r\n            if float(np.linalg.norm(f)) <= 8.0 * np.finfo(float).eps:\r\n                break\r\n            R_m = R_m @ rotation_matrix_from_vector(f)\r\n        else:\r\n            raise ValueError(f\"{label}: SO(3) mean failed to converge in the convex anchor ball\")\r\n        if float(np.linalg.norm(rotation_vector_from_matrix(anchor.T @ R_m))) >= radius:\r\n            raise ValueError(f\"{label}: SO(3) mean left the unique convex anchor ball\")\r\n    theta_m = rotation_vector_from_matrix(R_m)\r\n    if float(np.linalg.norm(theta_m)) >= chart_limit:\r\n        raise ValueError(f\"{label}: SO(3) mean reached the principal rotation-vector chart boundary\")\r\n    A = [_rotation_left_jacobian_inverse(psi) for psi in psis]\r\n    B = np.zeros((3, 3), dtype=float)\r\n    for a, w in zip(A, ws):\r\n        B += w * a\r\n    if float(np.min(np.linalg.eigvalsh(0.5 * (B + B.T)))) <= 0.0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\r\n            f\"{label}: intrinsic SO(3) mean tangent operator is singular; \"\r\n            \"ambiguous mean refused\"\r\n        )\r\n    B_inv = np.linalg.inv(B)\r\n    Jl_m_inv = _rotation_left_jacobian_inverse(theta_m)\r\n    G = []\r\n    for t, a, w in zip(thetas, A, ws):\r\n        Ghat = R_m @ (B_inv @ (w * a)) @ R_m.T\r\n        Jl_i = _rotation_right_jacobian(t).T\r\n        G.append(Jl_m_inv @ Ghat @ Jl_i)\r\n    return theta_m, tuple(G)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(\r\n    theta_bar: np.ndarray,\r\n    eccentricity: np.ndarray,\r\n) -> np.ndarray:\r\n    \"\"\"Return ``A(theta_bar, r_0) = d((R(theta_bar) - I) r_0) / d(theta_bar)``.\r\n\r\n    Exact closed-form derivative:\r\n        A(theta_bar, r_0) = -R(theta_bar) @ skew(r_0) @ J_r(theta_bar)\r\n    which reduces at ``theta_bar = 0`` to ``-skew(r_0)`` (i.e. ``theta x r_0``).\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    w = np.asarray(theta_bar, dtype=float).reshape(3)\r\n    r0 = np.asarray(eccentricity, dtype=float).reshape(3)\r\n    R = rotation_matrix_from_vector(w)\r\n    Jr = _rotation_right_jacobian(w)\r\n    return -(R @ _skew(r0) @ Jr)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef rotating_mpc_geometric_hessian(\r\n    theta_bar: np.ndarray,\r\n    eccentricity: np.ndarray,\r\n    slave_force: np.ndarray,\r\n) -> np.ndarray:\r\n    \"\"\"Return the symmetric 3x3 Hessian ``d(A(theta_bar, r_0)^T f_b) / d(theta_bar)``.\"\"\"\r\n    f_b = np.asarray(slave_force, dtype=float).reshape(3)\r\n    if float(np.linalg.norm(f_b)) <= 1.0e-30:\r\n        return np.zeros((3, 3), dtype=float)\r\n    w = np.asarray(theta_bar, dtype=float).reshape(3)\r\n    r0 = np.asarray(eccentricity, dtype=float).reshape(3)\r\n    if float(np.linalg.norm(r0)) <= 1.0e-30:\r\n        return np.zeros((3, 3), dtype=float)\r\n    H = np.zeros((3, 3), dtype=float)\r\n    h = 1.0e-6\r\n    for j in range(3):\r\n        wp = w.copy()\r\n        wm = w.copy()\r\n        wp[j] += h\r\n        wm[j] -= h\r\n        gp = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(wp, r0).T @ f_b\r\n        gm = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(wm, r0).T @ f_b\r\n        H[:, j] = (gp - gm) / (2.0 * h)\r\n    return 0.5 * (H + H.T)\r\n\r\n\r\nclass NonlinearRotatingMPCEntry:\r\n    \"\"\"One finite-rotation eccentric beam-shell coupling descriptor.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n    def __init__(\r\n        self,\r\n        *,\r\n        element_id: int,\r\n        slave_u_dofs: Tuple[int, int, int],\r\n        slave_theta_dofs: Tuple[int, int, int],\r\n        masters: Tuple[Tuple[Tuple[int, ...], Tuple[int, ...], float], ...],\r\n        eccentricity: np.ndarray,\r\n        theta_proj: Any,\r\n        label_prefix: str,\r\n        master_theta_projs: Tuple[Any, ...] = (),\r\n    ) -> None:\r\n        self.element_id = int(element_id)\r\n        self.slave_u_dofs = np.asarray(slave_u_dofs, dtype=np.intp)\r\n        self.slave_theta_dofs = np.asarray(slave_theta_dofs, dtype=np.intp)\r\n        self.masters = tuple(\r\n            (\r\n                np.asarray(m_u, dtype=np.intp),\r\n                np.asarray(m_theta, dtype=np.intp),\r\n                float(w),\r\n            )\r\n            for m_u, m_theta, w in masters\r\n        )\r\n        self.eccentricity = np.asarray(eccentricity, dtype=float).reshape(3).copy()\r\n        self.r_skew = _skew(self.eccentricity)\r\n        self.theta_proj = theta_proj.tocsr()\r\n        self.label_prefix = str(label_prefix)\r\n        self.master_theta_projs = tuple(p.tocsr() for p in master_theta_projs)\r\n\r\n    def master_rotation(self, u: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Linear average ``theta_lin = sum_i w_i theta_i`` (legacy relation).\"\"\"\r\n        theta_bar = np.zeros(3, dtype=float)\r\n        for _m_u, m_theta, w in self.masters:\r\n            theta_bar += w * u[m_theta]\r\n        return theta_bar\r\n\r\n    def master_rotations(self, u: np.ndarray) -> list:\r\n        \"\"\"Individual master rotation vectors ``theta_i``.\"\"\"\r\n        return [\r\n            np.asarray(u[m_theta], dtype=float).reshape(3).copy()\r\n            for _m_u, m_theta, _w in self.masters\r\n        ]\r\n\r\n    def mean_state(self, u: np.ndarray) -> Tuple[np.ndarray, Tuple[np.ndarray, ...]]:\r\n        \"\"\"Intrinsic weighted SO(3) mean of the master rotations and tangents.\"\"\"\r\n        thetas = self.master_rotations(u)\r\n        weights = [w for _m_u, _m_theta, w in self.masters]\r\n        return _so3_weighted_mean(thetas, weights, self.label_prefix)\r\n\r\n    def master_translation(self, u: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        u_bar = np.zeros(3, dtype=float)\r\n        for m_u, _m_theta, w in self.masters:\r\n            u_bar += w * u[m_u]\r\n        return u_bar\r\n\r\n\r\nclass NonlinearRotatingMPCMap:\r\n    \"\"\"State-dependent master-slave kinematic map for rotating eccentricities.\r\n\r\n    Given the reference linear constraint transformation ``T_linear`` (which\r\n    encodes ``u_b,lin = sum_i w_i u_{s,i} + theta_lin x r_0`` and\r\n    ``theta_b = theta_lin := sum_i w_i theta_{s,i}``), the nonlinear\r\n    kinematic map replaces the linearized lever-arm offset and the\r\n    non-objective averaged orientation with the intrinsic weighted SO(3)\r\n    mean ``R_m = mean(R_i, w_i)`` on the active rotating-offset route:\r\n\r\n        u_b(q)     = sum_i w_i u_{s,i}(q)\r\n                     + (R_m(q) - I) r_0\r\n        theta_b(q) = theta_m(q) = Log(R_m(q))\r\n\r\n    Under a common superposed rigid rotation ``Q`` the weighted deformed\r\n    attachment point and the slave orientation transform equivariantly,\r\n    ``x_b -> Q x_b`` and ``R_b -> Q R_b`` (adopted rigid-spin contract),\r\n    The correction to the linear displacement therefore subtracts\r\n    ``theta_lin x r_0``. The direct single-master route and identical-rotation\r\n    limit retain the legacy physical map. Zero Lagrange multipliers are used.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n\r\n    def __init__(\r\n        self,\r\n        T_linear: Any,\r\n        entries: Tuple[NonlinearRotatingMPCEntry, ...],\r\n    ) -> None:\r\n        from scipy import sparse\r\n\r\n        self.T_linear = sparse.csr_matrix(T_linear, dtype=float)\r\n        self.entries = tuple(entries)\r\n        self.total_dofs = int(self.T_linear.shape[0])\r\n        self.n_red = int(self.T_linear.shape[1])\r\n\r\n    @property\r\n    def active(self) -> bool:\r\n        return bool(self.entries)\r\n\r\n    def _slave_corrections(self, u: np.ndarray) -> list:\r\n        \"\"\"Per-entry slave translation/rotation corrections ``(d_u, d_theta)``.\r\n\r\n        Both corrections are evaluated from the supplied state only: master\r\n        rotations are never corrected by the map, so the result is a proper\r\n        function of the input state and forward/inverse application is\r\n        order-independent across chained entries.\r\n        \"\"\"\r\n        corrections = []\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            theta_lin = entry.master_rotation(u)\r\n            theta_m, _G = entry.mean_state(u)\r\n            d_theta = theta_m - theta_lin\r\n            d_u = _nonlinear_minus_linear_rotation(theta_m) @ entry.eccentricity\r\n            d_u = d_u + np.cross(d_theta, entry.eccentricity)\r\n            corrections.append((entry, d_u, d_theta))\r\n        return corrections\r\n\r\n    def apply_kinematics(self, u_linear: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Map a linear MPC displacement state ``u_lin`` to the nonlinear manifold.\"\"\"\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_linear, dtype=float).reshape(-1).copy()\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return u\r\n        for entry, d_u, d_theta in self._slave_corrections(u):\r\n            u[entry.slave_u_dofs] += d_u\r\n            u[entry.slave_theta_dofs] += d_theta\r\n        return u\r\n\r\n    def to_linear_displacement(self, u_nonlinear: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Exact inverse diffeomorphism mapping ``u`` back to linear MPC coordinates.\"\"\"\r\n        u_lin = np.asarray(u_nonlinear, dtype=float).reshape(-1).copy()\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return u_lin\r\n        for entry, d_u, d_theta in self._slave_corrections(u_lin):\r\n            u_lin[entry.slave_u_dofs] -= d_u\r\n            u_lin[entry.slave_theta_dofs] -= d_theta\r\n        return u_lin\r\n\r\n    def jacobian(self, u_current: np.ndarray) -> Any:\r\n        \"\"\"Return the state-dependent kinematic Jacobian ``J(u) = du / dq``.\"\"\"\r\n        from scipy import sparse\r\n\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return self.T_linear\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_current, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        row_blocks = []\r\n        col_blocks = []\r\n        data_blocks = []\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            if entry.theta_proj.nnz == 0 and all(\r\n                p.nnz == 0 for p in entry.master_theta_projs\r\n            ):\r\n                continue\r\n            theta_m, G_list = entry.mean_state(u)\r\n            A = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(theta_m, entry.eccentricity)\r\n            # M_proj = dtheta_m/dq = sum_i G_i dtheta_i/dq\r\n            M = None\r\n            for G, P in zip(G_list, entry.master_theta_projs):\r\n                if P.nnz == 0:\r\n                    continue\r\n                term = sparse.csr_matrix(G) @ P\r\n                M = term if M is None else (M + term)\r\n            if M is None:\r\n                continue\r\n            # slave translations: A @ M + skew(r_0) @ theta_proj\r\n            C_u = sparse.csr_matrix(A) @ M + sparse.csr_matrix(entry.r_skew) @ entry.theta_proj\r\n            # slave rotations: M - theta_proj\r\n            C_theta = M - entry.theta_proj\r\n            for rows, block in ((entry.slave_u_dofs, C_u), (entry.slave_theta_dofs, C_theta)):\r\n                coo = block.tocoo()\r\n                if coo.nnz and np.any(coo.data):\r\n", "numLines": 330, "startLine": 1181, "requestedOffset": 1181, "requestedLimit": 330, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": true}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "330 lines from C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": "(truncated)"}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-10f3ccfe-e6ea-5e42-8751-1793ba3e0386-0", "sessionId": "3290534b-2fa0-0d5b-1a6c-5caaf888f672", "turnId": "turn-efff736df4afd6e2b448c337e3fb4175", "createdAt": 1792351813840, "updatedAt": 1792351813840, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Now I understand the structure. The chart ValueError is raised in `_so3_weighted_mean` with several distinct messages:\n- \"outside the principal rotation-vector chart\" (input theta norm >= pi - 1e-6)\n- \"ambiguous shortest-geodesic SO(3) mean\" (two-master relative >= chart_limit)\n- \"requires a unique convex anchor ball of radius pi/4\"\n- \"failed to converge in the convex anchor ball\"\n- \"left the unique convex anchor ball\"\n- \"reached the principal rotation-vector chart boundary\"\n- \"tangent operator is singular\"\n\nThe brief: \"Do not catch arbitrary ValueError as kinematic admission; identify the specific internal domain failure.\" So I should introduce a typed exception, e.g. `class SO3MeanDomainError(ValueError)` raised by `_so3_weighted_mean` for these domain refusals (keeping ValueError subclass for backward compat), and in the DC line search catch exactly that type around `full_u(q_trial)` and treat the trial as inadmissible → backtrack (continue halving alpha); if no admissible improving step exists, keep the full Newton step? No — the brief says \"If no admissible improving step exists, return classified failure after rollback.\"\n\nWait, careful: the line search currently falls back to the full Newton step when no reduction improves (\"without a sufficient decrease the full Newton step is kept (unchanged behavior)\"). But if the full Newton step itself is inadmissible (full_u(q + delta) raises), then committing q += delta would produce a state whose next iteration's full_u(q) raises at the top of the loop — aborting the solve. The brief wants: an inadmissible trial is backtracked rather than aborting an otherwise valid committed state; if no admissible improving step exists, return classified failure after rollback.\n\nSo design:\n1. Add typed exception `SO3MeanDomainError(ValueError)` in corotational.py, raised by `_so3_weighted_mean` for the domain refusals (all the chart/convex/singular guards). Keep messages.\n2. In the DC Newton loop (rotating route), the trial evaluation points that can hit the domain: \n   - line search `full_u(q_trial)` and `_assemble_nonlinear_system` at u_trial\n   - the committed step `q += delta` — the next iteration's `u = full_u(q)` at loop top, and `reduce_force`, `jacobian`, etc.\n   \n   The minimal bounded change per brief: \"integrate bounded trial-domain rejection so an inadmissible trial is backtracked rather than aborting an otherwise valid committed state\". So in the line search, wrap the trial evaluation in try/except SO3MeanDomainError → treat as inadmissible (merit = inf), continue halving. And after the line search, before committing `q += delta`, check admissibility of the candidate step: if `full_u(q + delta[:-1])` raises SO3MeanDomainError, then... we can't commit. Options: backtrack further (beyond the 8 halvings?) or fail the attempt with a classified failure reason (e.g. \"trial_state_domain_refused\" / \"inadmissible_trial_state\") which then goes through the existing rollback path (q = q_attempt_start, discard candidate) and subdivision logic. Actually the failure path already handles rollback and subdivision: failure_reason set → discard candidate, restore q/lam, and if failure_reason == \"maximum_iterations_reached\" subdivide. For a domain refusal we'd want a distinct failure_reason like \"trial_domain_refused\" that also permits subdivision? Hmm. The brief: \"If no admissible improving step exists, return classified failure after rollback.\" So classified failure — a distinct status/reason. But should subdivision retry with a smaller span? That would be natural: a smaller control increment produces a smaller Newton step, likely admissible. But brief says \"return classified failure after rollback\" — for the case where no admissible improving step exists. Hmm, but within one Newton iteration, if all 8 halvings are inadmissible, we could classify failure. But maybe better: allow the subdivision machinery to handle it like maximum_iterations_reached? The brief explicitly says \"return classified failure after rollback\" — I'll implement: if the full Newton step is inadmissible and no halving is admissible, set failure_reason = \"trial_state_domain_refused\" (a new classified reason), which triggers rollback; and permit subdivision for this reason too (since a smaller span is the natural remedy and the existing cutback bound limits retries). Hmm — but \"return classified failure\" suggests not subdividing. Let me think about what's most compatible: The subdivision path requires failure_reason == \"maximum_iterations_reached\". If I add the domain refusal to the subdivision-eligible set, then the solve would cut back and retry with half span — bounded by max_control_cutbacks — and eventually either succeed or stop with minimum_control_step_reached/stopped_at_limit. That is \"backtracked rather than aborting\" at the control level too. But the brief says \"If no admissible improving step exists, return classified failure after rollback.\" I read this as: within the Newton attempt, when no admissible improving step exists (all line-search trials refused), don't commit garbage; roll back and return a classified failure (not a crash, not a false success). Whether the outer control loop then subdivides is the existing adaptive machinery. I think making domain refusal subdivision-eligible is the right \"bounded trial-domain rejection... backtracked rather than aborting an otherwise valid committed state\". But careful about scope creep and about the direct route.\n\nActually wait — for the direct route, single master, `_so3_weighted_mean` returns thetas[0] immediately with no chart check (len==1 returns before chart check? Let me check order: len(thetas)==1 → return thetas[0], (eye,). Yes, before chart_limit check. So direct never raises domain errors from the mean. The direct stop is a different defect: stopped_at_limit. Need per-accepted progress/iteration evidence.\n\nLet me now think about the direct stop. Direct full −0.20 stops at −0.1833 (older measurement) with min-step stop; current measurement says stopped_at_limit. The brief says \"diagnose current direct stop from per-accepted progress/iteration evidence, repair compatible Newton/globalization/state defect\". So I need an instrumented run of the direct full case printing per-accepted progress: accepted control value, lambda, iterations, cutbacks, failure reasons. The continuation test module has `_dc_case(coupling, -0.20, DC_STEPS)`. I can write a diagnostic script that runs the direct case with instrumentation (maybe via a monkeypatched hook or by reading result.steps history). Actually the result object has steps/snapshots with per-step records including iterations, control attempts, cutbacks. A diagnostic could run the direct case and dump the step records: control value, lambda, iterations, attempts, cutbacks, status. That's one ~90-120s run. Budget: 220s allocation. One direct instrumented run (~90s) + one focused verification pytest run (~90-120s?) Hmm. The full-target pytest run took 154s for 8 tests. A focused regression for trial refusal + preserved state would be cheaper if it uses a small fixture. But the full-target rerun to show the fix works would be expensive (~90s direct + ~113s interp = 200s+). That exceeds 220s with the diagnostic.\n\nBudget planning (220s max additional):\n- Diagnostic instrumented direct run: ~90-120s (bounded 120).\n- Then implement fixes.\n- Verification: focused regression tests (trial refusal + preserved state) — need to be cheap. Plus maybe one full-target interp run to show the ValueError no longer aborts (~113s). Total could exceed 220.\n\nHmm. \"Owner allocation maximum220 additional process-wall seconds; absolute ceiling 2250.623s\". Ledger now 2030.626. 2030.626+220 = 2250.626. So exactly 220s available.\n\nOptions: skip the separate diagnostic run and instead make the instrumented run itself the discriminating evidence — e.g., run the direct full case with per-accepted progress printing AND, since I can inspect the failure mode from existing evidence... Actually, do I already have per-accepted evidence? ep-dc-block-probe-direct-1 (88.75s) was a probe. Let me check what evidence exists for the direct stop — maybe stdout has per-step info already. Let me look at evidence/ep-dc-block-probe-direct-1 and ep-dc-limit-probe-2.\n\nAlso the brief says \"Prefer one discriminating instrumented full-target case, not sweeps or repeated unchanged solves.\"\n\nPlan for budget:\n1. Read existing evidence for direct stop details (free).\n2. Implement typed domain exception + line-search trial rejection + candidate admissibility check with classified failure (edits, free).\n3. One instrumented full-target direct run (~90-120s) that both diagnoses the direct stop (per-accepted progress) and tests... hmm, but if I fix before diagnosing, the diagnosis is contaminated. The brief says \"diagnose current direct stop from per-accepted progress/iteration evidence, repair compatible Newton/globalization/state defect\". So diagnosis first, then repair. But budget is tight.\n\nAlternative: one instrumented run of the CURRENT source for direct full target with per-accepted progress dump (~90s). Then implement repair for both direct defect and interp trial rejection. Then one focused verification: new regression tests (cheap, small fixture) + maybe rerun interp full target (~113s) — total 90+113 = 203s + regression ~15s = over 220? 90+113=203, plus a cheap regression 10-20s → ~220. Very tight. The direct full rerun to verify the direct fix wouldn't fit. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the direct fix can be verified within the same instrumented framework... Or maybe the direct stop diagnosis reveals something cheap to fix (e.g., a state/commit defect where an accepted state is rolled back incorrectly, or the min-span floor is too large relative to remaining span). Let me look at existing evidence first — ep-dc-block-probe-direct-1 stdout may already contain per-accepted info.\n\nLet me check the existing evidence outputs.", "summary": []}
