{"id": "reasoning-20785450-2053-580a-8f3b-f087671fe594-0", "sessionId": "3290534b-2fa0-0d5b-1a6c-5caaf888f672", "turnId": "turn-efff736df4afd6e2b448c337e3fb4175", "createdAt": 1792351837413, "updatedAt": 1792351837413, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Excellent — this is highly informative existing evidence, no new budget spent.\n\nDirect route diagnosis from ep-dc-step11-probe-1 (step 11, i.e., the increment from -0.1667 to -0.175... wait, ctrl -0.16666 step 10 accepted, step 11 target -0.175):\n\nThe Newton iteration at step 11 stalls: residual oscillates between ~4e3 and ~3e5, never converging, lam oscillates around 2.7-3.2. The constraint is satisfied exactly (0.0) every iteration — the control row is enforced — but the residual (reduced equilibrium) doesn't converge. The pattern: residual drops to ~4e3-7e3 then jumps back up to ~2.9e5. This looks like a limit cycle: Newton steps overshoot into plastic loading/unloading cycles (the fiber return map flipping states), or the line search keeps full steps that bounce.\n\nAlso ep-dc-block-probe-direct-1 fd_check: relative_error ≈ 2.0 on columns 14, 15, 23, 20 — that's a SIGN ERROR: relative error exactly 2.0 means FD vs analytic differ by sign ( (a-(-a))/a = 2 ). \"ResidualFD sign explains≈2 in probes\" was mentioned in HANDOVER §fourth run: \"ResidualFD sign explains≈2 in probes.\" Hmm, so the FD check itself had a sign convention issue — the probe's FD was computed with a sign error, so ≈2 is expected/explained, not a product defect. OK.\n\nKey numbers from ep-dc-limit-probe-2 (direct full, 12 scheduled steps to -0.20):\n- Steps 1-10: 6-7 iterations each, clean convergence, lam rises to 2.803 at ctrl -0.1667.\n- Step 11: 47 iters (with cutbacks), accepted at ctrl -0.175, lam 2.822.\n- Step 12: 93 iters, accepted at -0.17917, lam 2.819.\n- Step 13: 139 iters, accepted at -0.18125, lam 2.791.\n- Step 14: 146 iters, accepted at -0.18333, lam 2.668.\n- Then min control step reached: min_subincrement 6.51e-5, span/256... The remaining span is 0.01667 (from -0.18333 to -0.20), and min span = |target_increment|/(num_steps*256) = (0.20/12)/256 = 6.51e-5. Cutbacks: 11 total, max 8 per interval. So the interval got cut back 8 times: span 0.01667→...→6.5e-5-ish, each attempt failing with maximum_iterations_reached (25 iters per attempt), then minimum_control_step_reached.\n\nSo the direct route: near lam ≈ 2.82 (peak), the structure passes a limit point in the load-displacement curve? lam decreases from 2.822 to 2.668 as ctrl goes -0.175 → -0.18333. That's a descending branch — displacement control should handle that fine (it solves for lam). But Newton stalls: at step 11+ iterations explode (47, 93, 139, 146). The per-iteration probe (ep-dc-step11-probe-1) shows the residual oscillating: 4e3 → 2.9e5 → 1.1e5 → ... a limit cycle around lam ~2.7-3.2.\n\nThe oscillation pattern with period ~5: residual small (4e3) then big (2.9e5). This smells like plastic return-map flip-flopping: the Newton step moves q, fibers flip between elastic unloading and plastic loading, residual jumps. A merit-based line search exists (adaptive_control_subdivision route) — but wait, is the line search active here? The line search is inside `if adaptive_control_subdivision and np.any(delta):`. The step11 probe shows residual going UP after steps (4e3 → 2.9e5), meaning the line search accepted non-improving steps (falls back to full step when no reduction found). The line search only accepts alpha if merit_trial <= (1-1e-4*alpha)*merit_current; if no alpha improves, keeps full step — unchanged behavior. So at a plastic flip-flop point, no alpha improves merit, full step taken, cycle.\n\nHmm, but what's the actual defect to repair? The brief: \"diagnose current direct stop from per-accepted progress/iteration evidence, repair compatible Newton/globalization/state defect\". \n\nLook at the numbers again: at step 11, iteration residuals: 421031 → 151481 → 45600 → 13142 → 229014 (jump up!) → 64598 → 50227 → 7650 → 13010 → 290919 (jump) → ... The residual decreases nicely (421k→13k over 4 iters) then jumps to 229k. A Newton method converging quadratically would not jump back up unless the tangent is wrong or the state flips. The jump pattern after reaching ~5e3-1e4: 7650 → 13010 → 290919. Hmm.\n\nInteresting: the criterion is ~8e-5 * something. residual criterion: \"criterion\": 8e-5 — that's tolerance*reference presumably. Residual needs to get below ~8e-4? No wait: convergence requires residual_norm <= tolerance * reference. The printed \"criterion\" is presumably tolerance*reference ≈ 8e-5?? But residual is 4e3-3e5, many orders above. Hmm, that can't be — steps 1-10 converged in 6-7 iterations. Let me reconsider: maybe \"criterion\" printed is something else. residual 1.2e-7 at step 1 acceptance (from limit probe \"res\" field). So the convergence threshold is ~1e-7-ish scale? The step11 probe prints residual 421031 at iter 2 with criterion 1.2e-4. So threshold ≈ 1.2e-4?? But accepted res in limit probe is 1.2e-7... inconsistent. Maybe the probe's \"criterion\" is tolerance*reference where reference = max(|ref_vec|,1)... whatever. The point: residual oscillates 4e3..3e5 and never gets near threshold ~1e-4-ish? That means it's REALLY far. Hmm, but steps 1-10 got res to 1e-7 in 7 iterations. At step 11 the iteration stalls at res ~4e3 minimum — 7 orders above threshold.\n\nWait — maybe the probe prints residual BEFORE the iteration's assembly, i.e., at start. Sequence: iter2 res 421031, ..., decreasing to 13142 at iter5, then iter6 res 229014 — increased. So Newton is not monotone and cycles. The minimum reached ~4e3 (iter 15, 33) — still far above ~1e-4 criterion. So the Newton at step 11 genuinely cannot converge: it's cycling in a plastic flip-flop loop.\n\nThis is the classic elastoplastic snap-through-ish behavior at a plastic hinge: near the peak lam 2.82, the cross-section fully plasticizes; the tangent is nearly singular in the controlled direction... The augmented smallest singular value at terminal: 0.0106 vs largest 8.5e9 — condition ~8e11. Hmm, dimensional. The tiny step: dq_norm 2.66e-4, dlam -0.0061, trial residual 6.35 (small!), trial uz -0.18327. Interesting: a tiny step from the terminal state gives trial residual 6.35 — much smaller than the 4e3-3e5 residuals in the step11 probe. Hmm, different points though.\n\nActually wait — look at \"tiny_step_trial_residual\": 6.35. If the convergence criterion is ~1e-4 * reference and reference is maybe ~1e5-ish (applied scale 61917), then threshold ~6. Residual 6.35 is just above. So at the terminal accepted state (step 14, ctrl -0.18333, lam 2.668), a tiny augmented step yields trial residual 6.35, close to convergence but the iteration count (25 max) gets consumed cycling.\n\nLet me reconsider the step11 probe: \"criterion\": 8e-5. If that's tolerance*reference... tolerance is 1e-8 (controls12/40/1e-8 per brief — 12 steps, 40 iterations, 1e-8 tolerance). reference = max(norm(ref_vec),1). ref_vec = reduce_force(u, F_const + max(|lam|,1)*F_prop). Applied scale 61917 N. So reference ~ 61917*2.8 ≈ 1.7e5. tolerance*reference = 1e-8*1.7e5 = 1.7e-3. But probe says criterion 1.2e-4. Hmm, close-ish order. Whatever — the residual 4e3 >> 1.7e-3. So at step 11, Newton cycles at residual ~1e4, i.e., equilibrium cannot be found for ctrl = -0.175?? But the limit probe says step 11 WAS accepted at ctrl -0.175 with res 1.44e-7 after 47 iterations (including cutbacks — so some subdivided subinterval succeeded). OK so with subdivision, it eventually found equilibrium at -0.175 (47 iters across attempts). Then -0.17917 (93), -0.18125 (139), -0.18333 (146). Progress per accepted increment is slowing dramatically: each 1/12 step needs ~8x more work, and after -0.18333 even the minimum span (6.5e-5) fails.\n\nSo the physical picture: beyond uz ≈ -0.1833, the structure is at/near a plastic collapse point where displacement control itself hits a limit (the controlled displacement cannot increase without... hmm, in displacement control a limit point in load is fine; what stops DC is a snap-back in the control variable or genuinely no equilibrium nearby).\n\nBut the brief says: \"Do not assert physical collapse from failure to follow force/DC path.\" And \"repair compatible Newton/globalization/state defect\". So is there a defect, or is -0.1833 a genuine physical limit? The residual cycling pattern (4e3 → 2.9e5 → ...) suggests the Newton iteration is bouncing between plastic states — a numerical globalization defect rather than pure physics. A proper line search on a merit function with plasticity-consistent (consistent tangent) assembly should converge near a regular equilibrium. The oscillation residual jumping UP by 30x after reaching 1e4 suggests the line search fails and takes a full step into a different plastic branch.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me reconsider: is the line search even active in these probes? The line search was added in the fifth run (\"residual backtracking IS implemented in nonlinear_static.py\"). The probes ep-dc-step11-probe-1 were run at 18:34 (before the fifth run's changes at ~19:00+?). Timeline: ep-dc-step11-probe-1 at 18:34, slice3-dc-subdivision 18:38, ep-dc-limit-probe-2 18:41, block probes 18:43/18:45. The fifth run (residual backtracking) came after 18:47 (slice3-continuation-achievable-1) — supervisor-dc-direct-globalized-1 at 18:59 failed with TypeError. So ALL these direct-route probes predate the residual line search! The current direct measurement is only \"stopped_at_limit\" from supervisor-domain-dc-full-1 (19:25, current source). So the current direct stop reason is unknown in detail — the brief says \"diagnose current direct stop from per-accepted progress/iteration evidence\" — meaning I need fresh evidence from the CURRENT source.\n\nSo I do need one instrumented run of the current direct full target with per-accepted progress + per-iteration residuals. ~90-120s. That leaves ~100-130s for verification.\n\nLet me now think about what the repair might be, so the instrumented run can also test it... No — diagnose first. But budget is tight: 220s total. Diagnostic run ~90-120s. Then edits. Then verification: focused regression (trial refusal, ~10-30s with a small fixture) + one full-target interp run (~113s)? 120+30+113 = 263 > 220. Doesn't fit. \n\nAlternative: make the diagnostic run cover BOTH routes? No, one child at a time, and direct alone is ~90s.\n\nPrioritize per brief: \"Prefer one discriminating instrumented full-target case, not sweeps.\" The outcome requires: (1) diagnose direct stop, (2) repair, (3) integrate trial-domain rejection, (4) focused regression for trial refusal and preserved state. Verification of the interp fix (no more ValueError abort) is directly checkable via the full-target interp case, but expensive. The focused regression can use a small fixture that triggers chart refusal in a trial — e.g., a tiny model with two masters where a Newton trial pushes master rotations beyond the chart. Constructing that cheaply might be hard; alternatively a unit-level regression: call the DC block with a monkeypatched rotating map whose apply_kinematics raises SO3MeanDomainError for trials beyond a threshold, verifying (a) the solve backtracks/returns classified failure, (b) committed state preserved (rollback), (c) no constitutive mutation on refused trials. That's cheap and focused — good.\n\nBudget plan (220s):\n1. Instrumented direct full-target run on CURRENT source: ~90-120s (timeout 120). This gives per-accepted progress + per-iteration residual trace for the current globalization.\n2. Implement: typed SO3MeanDomainError + line-search trial rejection + candidate admissibility/classified failure; plus direct-route repair based on diagnosis.\n3. Focused regression pytest (new test, small/monkeypatched fixture): ~10-30s.\n4. If budget remains (~70-100s): one full-target interp run to verify no-abort + state preser