gress. How to instrument without editing product? Write a diagnostic script that imports the test module's fixture builder and wraps `nonlinear_static._assemble_nonlinear_system`? The per-iteration residual is computed inside the loop; I can't easily print it without hooks. Options:\n- Copy the DC loop instrumentation via monkeypatching factorize to record delta? Complex.\n- Simpler: the result object already records per-step records (steps with iterations, control attempts, cutbacks, lam, control value, residual?). The limit probe printed per-step: step, lam, iters, res, ctrl, eps_p. That came from result.steps presumably (NonlinearStaticStep records). So a diagnostic script can just run the direct full case on CURRENT source and dump result.steps + status + stop_reason + adaptation info. That gives per-accepted progress/iteration evidence for the current globalization. Plus, to see line-search behavior, I can count `control_line_search_reductions` if exposed... let me check what's in the result info. The block probe printed \"adaptation\": attempts, cutbacks, max_cutbacks_per_interval, min_subincrement, applied. So info includes control_attempts, control_cutbacks, etc.\n\nFor per-iteration detail (whether line search finds improvements), I could monkeypatch `corotational`... no. Alternative: wrap `nonlinear_static.factorize`? It's imported inside the function (`from ... import factorize`?). Let me check how factorize is referenced in the DC loop — `handle = factorize(aug, MatrixClass.GENERAL, signature=...)`. If `factorize` is a module-level import in nonlinear_static, I can monkeypatch `nonlinear_static.factorize` with a wrapper that records the rhs/solution norms. That gives per-iteration delta norms and residual rhs norms. Combined with a wrapper on `_assemble_nonlinear_system` counting calls (line-search trials vs main iterations), I can infer line-search activity: calls per iteration > 1 means line search ran.\n\nLet me write the diagnostic to record per factorize call: iteration signature, ||rhs||, ||delta||, |dlam|, and per assemble call count. And at the end dump result steps/status/stop_reason/adaptation. That's a solid discriminating instrument.\n\nWhere to put the script: diagnostics/ under the task folder (.development/.../diagnostics/ exists — ep_dc_block_probe.py etc. are there). Owned boundary: brief owns src files + tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3*.py. Diagnostics in the task folder are task artifacts — previous runs added diagnostics scripts there (ep_dc_block_probe.py). The brief says \"Preserve all other dirty work, original backups, failed evidence and root assessment tools\" — adding a new diagnostic script in the task diagnostics folder follows the established pattern (run_assessments.py, capture_candidate.py exist at task root). I'll add `.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/diagnostics/dc_final_repair_probe.py`.\n\nBudget: timeout 120 for the diagnostic. Direct full previously took ~88.75s (block probe, which also did FD checks). A leaner probe might take ~60-90s. OK.\n\nLet me check DC_STEPS, DC_MAX_ITER, DC_TOL and the _build_ep_model fixture in the continuation test, and how the limit probe script was written (to reuse). Let me look at diagnostics/ep_dc_limit_probe.py.", "summary": []}
