{"id": "reasoning-e6cb37b2-af32-5c95-9c1e-5f48cac6e911-0", "sessionId": "3290534b-2fa0-0d5b-1a6c-5caaf888f672", "turnId": "turn-efff736df4afd6e2b448c337e3fb4175", "createdAt": 1792351868641, "updatedAt": 1792351868641, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Wait, there's a bug: `_ProbingHandle` is referenced inside `probing_factorize` before the class is defined — actually in Python, the class is defined after `probing_factorize` but before it's called, so it resolves fine at call time... no — `_ProbingHandle` is a local name in `main()` defined after `probing_factorize`. At call time, the name lookup will find it in the enclosing scope. Fine.\n\nBut the \"iter\" record prints before solve with None fields — redundant. Simplify: just print the solve record with line_search_trials included. Let me restructure: move the trials count into the solve record. The \"iter\" print is useless. Let me edit: remove the print in probing_factorize, and add line_search_trials to the solve record. But careful: the trials counted are those since the LAST solve — the line search happens after the factorize/solve of the previous iteration... Sequence per iteration: assemble (tangent=True) at top → factorize → solve → line search trials (assemblies tangent=False) → commit → next iteration assemble. So at the time of solve for iteration k, the residual-call count includes trials from iteration k-1's line search. That's fine — record them as \"trials_before_this_solve\" or just attribute to the previous iteration. I'll record the count at solve time and label it \"line_search_trials_since_prev_solve\".\n\nLet me rewrite the script more cleanly.", "summary": []}
