elopment/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/evidence/dc-final-repair-probe-direct-1\" && wc -l stdout.txt && head -5 stdout.txt && echo ... && tail -25 stdout.txt && cat stderr.txt | head -5", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-b8f46fe9-3ee9-5601-b534-c934bc5fb8ba-0", "sessionId": "3290534b-2fa0-0d5b-1a6c-5caaf888f672", "turnId": "turn-efff736df4afd6e2b448c337e3fb4175", "createdAt": 1792352085796, "updatedAt": 1792352085796, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Excellent — this is a decisive diagnosis. The current direct run reaches an exact 3-cycle:\n\n- iter A: residual 1058.09, dq_norm 7.136e-5, dlam +9.227e-4, 8 line-search trials (no improvement found → full step taken)\n- iter B: residual 102.489, dq 1.283e-5, dlam -1.723e-7... wait dlam -1.72e-7? No: -0.000172287... = -1.72e-4, 1 trial\n- iter C: residual 76.526, dq 6.826e-5, dlam -8.797e-4, 3 trials\n- back to iter A: residual 1058.09... identical to machine precision.\n\nThe state cycles EXACTLY (residual values repeat to 10+ digits). The Newton iteration is trapped in a period-3 limit cycle. The line search runs 8 trials at iter A (all fail the sufficient-decrease test), so the FULL step is kept → jumps back into the cycle.\n\nAlso note iter 397: residual 1.01e-7 (converged-level!) with constraint_rhs -6.51e-5 — that's a subdivision boundary: after a failed attempt, span halved, new target. Actually constraint_rhs -6.51e-5 = the min subincrement. Interesting: at n=397 the residual was 1e-7 — essentially converged — with a fresh constraint. Hmm, that looks like an accepted subincrement publish... Actually no, solve records are per Newton iteration. n=397: residual 1.0135e-07 with constraint -6.51e-5: this is the first iteration of a new subdivided attempt starting from the last accepted state (residual small at start), target increment -6.51e-5 (the min span). Then n=398: residual 367.96 — after taking the step for that tiny span, residual jumped to 368! A 6.5e-5 control increment from a converged state throws the residual from 1e-7 to 368?? That means the tangent is inconsistent with the residual at this point — an infinitesimal step produces a huge residual jump. That's the classic consistent-tangent defect OR... wait, but then it cycles 1058→102→76→1058.\n\nHmm, actually residual 368 after a tiny step suggests the assembled residual at the new point is genuinely large — i.e., the equilibrium surface here is extremely sensitive (near a snap-back / fold in the reduced system?) or the tangent used is wrong (e.g., plastic algorithmic tangent flipping).\n\nKey observation: the cycle is EXACT — same residuals to 13 significant digits. This means deterministic flip-flopping between plastic states: the trial crosses a yield surface, the return map flips a fiber's elastic/plastic status, and the Newton step undoes it, forever. The line search merit test never passes (8 trials at iter A), so full steps are taken.\n\nWhat's the compatible repair? The brief: \"repair compatible Newton/globalization/state defect and integrate bounded trial-domain rejection so an inadmissible trial is backtracked rather than aborting... If no admissible improving step exists, return classified failure after rollback.\"\n\nSo: when the line search finds NO improving alpha (all 8 halvings fail the sufficient-decrease test), currently it keeps the full step (unchanged behavior fallback). The repair: treat this as a failed iteration — do NOT commit the non-improving full step; set a classified failure reason and roll back to the attempt start, then the existing bounded subdivision handles it (smaller span → smaller step → maybe admissible/improving). If subdivision is exhausted → classified failure with preserved committed state.\n\nThis directly addresses the cycle: at iter A, no alpha improves → fail attempt → subdivide. The subdivision continues halving; at some span the Newton step from the accepted state may converge (as it did for steps 11-14 in the old run). Eventually if even min span fails → stopped_at_limit with classified reason (line_search_failed / no_improving_step). The solve then returns the last committed state — which is what \"return classified failure after rollback\" means.\n\nBut wait — will that make the direct full target COMPLETE at -0.20? Not necessarily. If the physics genuinely has no equilibrium beyond -0.1833 (or the path is a snap-back), the solve will still stop at limit, but now quickly and with a classified reason instead of burning 40 iterations × 8 trials cycling. The brief says: \"Do not assert physical collapse from failure to follow force/DC path\" — so stopping at limit with a classified reason is an acceptable outcome; the deliverable is the repaired globalization behavior (no cycling, bounded work, preserved state).\n\nHmm, but hold on. Let me reconsider whether \"no improving alpha → fail attempt\" is right vs \"no improving alpha → take the BEST trial anyway (min merit)\". The Armijo-style test with 8 halvings: if none passes, options:\n(a) keep full step (current) → cycle.\n(b) fail attempt → subdivide (my proposal).\n(c) take the alpha with minimal merit among trials.\n\nThe brief's language: \"If no admissible improving step exists, return classified failure after rollback.\" — supports (b). Also \"repair compatible Newton/globalization/state defect\" — the cycle is the defect.\n\nBut caution about compatibility: does (b) break the currently-passing cases? The half-target -0.10 cases pass currently. In those runs, did any iteration have zero improving alphas? If yes, under (b) those attempts would fail and subdivide — the solve would still likely complete (subdivision retries with half span), but with more cutbacks, and if cutbacks exceed 8 per interval → stop. Risk. From the probe: early iterations show line_search_trials_prev 8 at n=2 (first postyield step: 8 trials, no improvement, full step kept) — yet the solve continued and converged that step (n=3: residual 169.65, 1 trial; n=4: 156.39; n=5: 6.36e-5 → converged). Wait — n=2 had 8 trials and kept the full step, and the iteration still eventually converged (by n=5). So under design (b), that first step would have been classified failed and subdivided — the step would retry with half span. Would it converge? Probably yes (smaller step). But it changes the accepted path/iteration counts for ALL currently passing tests, and their per-snapshot assertions (wrench balance etc.) should still hold since they check equilibrium, not path. But run time may increase (more attempts). And the EP half tests passed in 26-27s; with extra subdivisions maybe more.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually let me re-read n=2: residual_rhs 441098 — that's the first iteration of step 2? n=1: residual 0, constraint -1/60 → first iteration of step 1 (from zero state). n=2: residual 441098, 8 trials. So during step 1, iteration 2, the line search ran 8 trials without improvement and kept the full step; then n=3 residual 169.65 — the full step REDUCED the residual from 441098 to 169.65! Wait, that contradicts \"no improving alpha\". If alpha=1 (the full step) reduced merit from 441098-scale to 169-scale, then the alpha=1 trial SHOULD have passed the sufficient decrease test...\n\nUnless the merit at trial includes the constraint term and normalization: merit = res/(tol*ref) + constraint/(tol*target_scale). Hmm, with tol=1e-8, res 441098/(1e-8*ref)... The merit test uses relative decrease: merit_trial <= (1-1e-4*alpha)*merit_current. If alpha=1 trial had merit 169-ish vs current 441098-ish, it passes easily. So why 8 trials?\n\nLook again: \"line_search_trials_prev: 8\" at n=2 means 8 residual assemblies happened between solve n=1 and solve n=2. The line search after solve n=1 ran 8 trials (alpha=1, 0.5, ..., 0.0078) — all failing? Then full step kept, and n=2's residual is 441098. Hmm wait, n=2's residual_rhs 441098 is the residual at the START of iteration 2, i.e., AFTER the step from n=1 was committed. n=1: residual 0 (initial state, no external load? lam starts 0). The step from n=1: dq 0.0678, dlam 1.507 — a HUGE load factor jump (from 0 to 1.5). The line search trialed 8 alphas; if all failed the test, full step kept → residual 441098 at n=2. Then the n=2 solve gives a correction; 1 trial at alpha=1 passed (residual 169.65 at n=3).\n\nSo at n=1→n=2: the merit test failed for all alphas even though the full step landed at residual 441098 vs current merit... at n=1, merit_current: residual_norm=0 → merit = 0 + constraint_error/(tol*target_scale). constraint at n=1: the constraint is exactly satisfied? constraint_rhs=-1/60 is the target for the solve. Hmm, merit_current at iteration 1: residual_norm=0, constraint_error = |target - current| = 1/60. So merit_current = 0 + (1/60)/(1e-8 * target_scale). target_scale presumably ~0.20 or 1... merit_current is huge positive. merit_trial for alpha=1: residual 441098/(1e-8*ref) + constraint_trial/(...). ref ~ norm of reduced reference forces ~ maybe 1e5-1e6. 441098/(1e-8*1e6)=44.1. constraint_trial/(1e-8*0.2)... if constraint_trial ~0 → merit_trial ≈ 44. merit_current ≈ (0.0167)/(1e-8*0.2) = 8.3e6. So merit_trial=44 <= (1-1e-4)*8.3e6 — PASSES easily. So alpha=1 should have been accepted at n=1 with only 1 trial. But 8 trials ran...\n\nHmm wait, maybe the 8 trials belong to the line search AFTER solve n=1, i.e., between n=1 and n=2 — yes that's what I said. If alpha=1 passed, the loop breaks at line_reduction=0 → 1 trial. But 8 trials ran → alpha=1 FAILED the test?? With merit_current=8.3e6 and merit_trial=44? Impossible... unless merit_current at n=1 was NOT huge. Let me recompute: at iteration 1 (first iteration of step 1), the state is the initial state: u=0, lam=0. residual = reduce_force(u, F_ext - F_int) = reduce_force(0, 0) = 0. constraint = target - current = -1/60 - 0 → constraint_error = 1/60. reference = max(||ref_vec||, 1): ref_vec = reduce_force(u, F_const + max(|lam|,1)*F_prop) with lam=0 → max(|lam|,1)=1 → ref_vec = reduce_force(0, F_prop) = reduced proportional load ~ T^T F_prop... applied load 2000 N/node × ... scale 61917 mentioned. So reference ≈ 61917. residual_norm=0 → first term 0. merit_current = 0 + (1/60)/(1e-8 * target_scale). What's target_scale? Probably max(|target|, something) = 0.2. So (0.0167)/(2e-9) = 8.3e6. Yes huge.\n\nmerit_trial at alpha=1: residual_trial = 441098?? Wait no — the trial at alpha=1 evaluates the state AFTER the full step, giving residual 441098 (that's what n=2 shows). merit_trial = 441098/(1e-8*61917) + constraint_trial/(1e-8*0.2) = 441098/6.19e-4 = 7.1e8 + ... That's MUCH BIGGER than merit_current=8.3e6! Because the residual term normalization 1e-8*reference is TINY (6.2e-4), so residual 441098 → merit 7.1e8, while the constraint term 1/60 → 8.3e6. So the full step INCREASED merit massively (because the huge residual dominates). All 8 halvings also failed? At alpha=0.0078, the trial state is near the initial state: residual small (~alpha*441098?), constraint ~ (1-alpha)*1/60 → merit ≈ (1-0.0078)*8.3e6 ≈ 8.27e6 vs required <= (1-1e-4*0.0078)*8.3e6 = 8.3e6*(1-7.8e-7). Hmm: merit_trial ≈ 8.27e6 < 8.3e6*(1-7.8e-7) ≈ 8.29999e6. So 8.27e6 <= 8.30e6 → PASSES?! Then alpha=0.0078 would be accepted... but 8 trials ran, meaning even alpha=0.0078 failed. Hmm, maybe residual at small alpha isn't linear; the residual at alpha=0.0078: the state is nearly initial; F_int at tiny displacement ~ K*u ~ tiny; residual ~ lam_trial*F_prop_red - F_int_red. lam_trial = 1.507*0.0078 = 0.0118. residual ≈ 0.0118*61917 = 730 (reduced). merit ≈ 730/6.19e-4 = 1.2e6 + constraint term (1-0.0078)*8.3e6=8.24e6 → total 9.4e6 > 8.3e6*(1-7.8e-7). FAILS. Yes! Because the residual term at alpha→0 doesn't vanish relative to the constraint term — the load factor must increase to satisfy equilibrium, but the constraint (control) is satisfied at rate alpha... The merit function combines two terms that scale differently; near the initial state, the constraint term dominates merit_current, and any trial that doesn't move the control to target keeps a big constraint term while picking up residual. So all trials fail, full step kept. OK — so the line search's merit test is structurally unable to accept any step at iteration 1 (from zero state with lam=0). That's why 8 trials ran and the full step was kept. Fine — the fallback worked there and the step eventually converged.\n\nNow the cycle at the end: iter A residual 1058.09, 8 trials (all fail), full step → iter B residual 102.49 (merit DECREASED massively! 1058→102). Wait, if the full step decreased the residual from 1058 to 102, the alpha=1 trial should have PASSED the test (merit_trial ≈ 102-scale < (1-1e-4)*1058-scale). Unless the constraint term... constraint_rhs at iter B is 0.0 — the constraint is satisfied. Hmm, but wait: the trial evaluations happen AFTER solve A. The trial at alpha=1 evaluates the same state that iteration B starts from — residual 102.49, constraint 0. merit_trial(B-state) vs merit_current(A-state): A-state residual 1058, constraint 0 (constraint_rhs at A is 0.0). So merit decreases 10x → alpha=1 passes → 1 trial, not 8!\n\nBut the trace shows 8 trials at iter A (the 1058-residual iterations). Contradiction... unless the 8 trials at \"iter A\" records belong to the line search of the PREVIOUS iteration. Let me re-read: at solve n, \"line_search_trials_prev\" = trials since previous solve = trials run by the line search AFTER solve n-1. So for the iteration starting with residual 1058 (call it state A, solve k), trials_prev=8 means the line search after solve k-1 ran 8 trials. Solve k-1 was the iteration starting at residual 76.526 (state C): its line search trialed 8 alphas, all failed, kept full step → landed in state A (1058). Then solve at A: 1 trial (trials_prev=1 at next solve, the 102.49 one) → alpha=1 passed (76.5→102.49?? that's an INCREASE 76.5→102.5... hmm no: trial after solve at A lands in state B with residual 102.49; merit A→B: 1058→102 decreasing, passes at alpha=1 → 1 trial. Then solve at B (102.49): 3 trials → landed at C (76.526): 102.49→76.5 decreasing... but 3 trials ran meaning alpha=1 and 0.5 failed, 0.25 passed? Then solve at C (76.526): 8 trials, all failed → full step → A (1058). So the cycle: C --(full step, no improvement)--> A --(alpha=1)--> B --(alpha~0.25)--> C.\n\nSo the non-improving step is C→A: from residual 76.5, the full Newton step lands at 1058 (worse), and ALL 8 halvings also fail to improve merit. From C, no admissible improving step exists → currently the full step is committed anyway → cycle forever.\n\nThis is EXACTLY the brief's case: \"If no admissible improving step exists, return classified failure after rollback.\" So the repair: when no alpha passes the sufficient-decrease test, do not commit the full step; classify the attempt failed and roll back → subdivision halves the span → retry from the last committed state. Bounded by cutback limits → eventually classified stop with preserved state.\n\nBut wait — the n=1 case also had \"no improving alpha\" (8 trials) and the full step eventually led to convergence. Under the new rule, that attempt would fail and subdivide. Is that bad? At the initial state, the first attempt of step 1 would fail → subdivide → span 1/120 → the first iteration from zero state: same structure — line search from merit dominated by constraint term... Actually with a smaller span, the Newton step is smaller (dlam ~ span-proportional? The augmented solve gives dlam such that control reaches target: for span 1/120, dlam ≈ half). The trial at alpha=1: residual ~ half of 441098, constraint satisfied → merit ≈ 2.2e8 vs current 4.2e6 (constraint 1/120 → 4.2e6). Still fails. All alphas fail again → fail → subdivide again... until cutbacks_this_step hits max_control_cutbacks (8) → stop!? That would BREAK step 1 entirely! The direct full target would stop at the very first step with min span. Disaster.\n\nHmm wait, is that right? At iteration 1 from the zero state: merit_current = 0 + constraint_error/(tol*target_scale). Any trial with alpha<1 keeps constraint_error ≈ (1-alpha)*span → merit ≈ (1-alpha)*span/(2e-9). For alpha=0.99: constraint term ≈ 0.01*1/60/(2e-9)=8.3e4... plus residual term at alpha=0.99: ~0.99*441098/6.19e-4=7e8. Fails. alpha=0.0078: computed above 9.4e6 vs 8.3e6 — fails barely. Smaller alpha: alpha=2^-8=0.0039: residual term ≈ 0.0039*441098/6.19e-4 = 2.8e6? Hmm wait, is the trial residual linear in alpha? At the initial state with lam=0, the trial state has u_trial = full step displacement * alpha, lam_trial = 1.507*alpha. Residual ≈ lam_trial*F_prop - F_int(u_trial) ≈ alpha*(1.507*F_prop - K*du) — linear in alpha for small alpha. So merit_trial(alpha) ≈ alpha*R + (1-alpha)*C where R = 441098/(6.19e-4) = 7.1e8 and C = 8.3e6. merit_current = C = 8.3e6. Test: alpha*R + (1-alpha)*C <= (1-1e-4*alpha)*C → alpha*R <= -1e-4*alpha*C → R <= -1e-4*C: NEVER (R>0). So indeed, from the zero state, NO alpha ever passes. The merit function is structurally broken at iteration 1 of every attempt starting from an unloaded state: the residual term R is huge relative to C because the load factor must jump.\n\nHmm, so \"no improving step → fail attempt\" would break the first step of every fresh solve. Unless... the line search should only trigger when it can help. Note the code: `if adaptive_control_subdivision and np.any(delta):` and `if merit_current > 0.0:`. At iteration 1, merit_current > 0 (constraint term). Hmm.\n\nSo the repair needs to be more careful. The real defect in the cycle: C→A where the full step increases merit and no alpha improves. But at iteration 1, no alpha improves either, yet committing the full step is correct and converges.\n\nWhat distinguishes them? At iteration 1, the constraint is unsatisfied (constraint_error = span) — the step is primarily a control move; the merit is dominated by the constraint term, and the trial residual is an unavoidable consequence of load increase. In the cycle, constraint_error = 0 (control satisfied; constraint_rhs=0.0) — the iteration is pure equilibrium correction; merit is pure residual; an increasing step is a genuine non-convergence signal.\n\nSo a compatible repair: when no alpha improves the merit, distinguish:\n- If the current constraint error is (near) zero — i.e., the merit is essentially the residual — then no improving step means the Newton attempt is cycling: classify failure (rollback + subdivision).\n- If the constraint is not yet satisfied (control move pending), keep the existing full-step fallback (unchanged behavior).\n\nAlternatively: only fail the attempt when the full step's TRIAL residual is worse than current AND constraint is already satisfied. Hmm.\n\nActually, simpler and more principled: the line search's purpose (per the code comment) is to prevent overshoot of the load factor from a converged state: \"from a converged state the first postyield Newton step can overshoot the load factor catastrophically.\" The cycle case: current state C has residual 76.5 (not converged but small), constraint satisfied. No alpha improves → cycling.\n\nLet me think about what a classical globalization would do: line search on the residual norm; if no decrease, declare non-convergence of the attempt (fail → subdivide). The iteration-1 issue is an artifact of the merit including the constraint term with a tiny denominator making R enormous. Actually wait — for a pure equilibrium iteration (constraint=0), merit = res/(tol*ref). The test: res_trial <= (1-1e-4*alpha)*res_current. In the cycle at C: res 76.5; trials at alpha=1: 1058 (worse), 0.5: ?, ... all worse. So fail → subdivide. At iteration 1: constraint term dominates; the test compares merit including constraint. Since the constraint term at trial (1-alpha)*span... hmm.\n\nOption: normalize the two merit terms separately and require improvement in the TOTAL, but when the current constraint error is above the convergence threshold (control not yet reached), skip the failure classification (keep full-step fallback). When constraint error is already within tolerance (pure equilibrium iteration), no-improvement → classified failure.\n\nThat's a clean, compatible rule:\n- The line search keeps its current acceptance test.\n- NEW: if no alpha passed AND the current state already satisfies the control constraint (constraint_error <= tolerance*target_scale, the same criterion as convergence), then the full step is not committed; failure_reason = \"line_search_no_improvement\" (classified), break → rollback → subdivision (bounded) → eventually classified stop with preserved committed state.\n- If no alpha passed but the constraint is not yet satisfied, keep the full step (unchanged behavior — preserves iteration-1 and any control-move-dominant iterations).\n\nIn the cycle: C has constraint 0 (satisfied) → fail → subdivide. The subdivided attempt starts from the last ACCEPTED state (rollback to attempt start), with half span. The Newton step from there is smaller; may converge or again hit the cycle; bounded by 8 cutbacks → min span → classified stop. The direct full target may still stop before -0.20 — but now with bounded work, a classified reason, and quickly (no 40×8-trial cycles). And per the brief, that's acceptable: \"If no admissible improving step exists, return classified failure after rollback\" and \"Do not assert physical collapse from failure to follow force/DC path.\"\n\nHmm, but wait — will the direct case actually complete -0.20 with this? Unknown. The old pre-line-search run accepted increments up to -0.18333 with heavy iteration counts. With the new rule, attempts that cycle fail fast and subdivide; subdivision may find equilibrium increments (as before, steps 11-14 accepted after cutbacks). Whether it reaches -0.20 depends on physics. We'll measure once.\n\nAlso the trial-domain rejection (interp): same infrastructure — a trial that raises SO3MeanDomainError is inadmissible (merit +inf). If ALL alphas are inadmissible OR fail the test → same classified failure path (with the constraint-satisfied guard). For interp, the ValueError occurred in the line search — with rejection, the line search will try smaller alphas; the committed state is admissible, so small alpha is admissible; if the admissible small alphas don't improve merit → classified failure → rollback/subdivision instead of abort. \n\nNow — the commit guard: after the line search, delta may be scaled by alpha or kept full. The endpoint q+delta could still be inadmissible if... the line search at alpha=1 passed the merit test but the endpoint is chart-admissible by construction (the trial evaluation full_u(q_trial) succeeded — the same endpoint). If no alpha passed and we keep the full step (constraint-unsatisfied case), the endpoint was never evaluated → could be inadmissible → next loop top full_u(q) raises → abort. So I still need the commit guard for that path: evaluate full_u(q+delta[:-1]) before committing; if SO3MeanDomainError → classified failure (trial_state_domain_refused) → rollback/subdivision. This is an extra apply_kinematics per iteration — cheap (only kinematics, no assembly).\n\nAlso the initial `u = full_u(q)` at loop top and other full_u calls (candidate_u for contact) — those operate on committed/accepted states which are admissible... except after a committed full-step fallback that we now guard. With the commit guard, every committed q is verified admissible. Good.\n\nWhat about `reduce_force`, `jacobian` etc. raising SO3MeanDomainError at the committed state? Committed states are admissible (verified), and mean_state is called on u — the same u that passed apply_kinematics. Fine.\n\nAlso: should the classified failure reasons be subdivision-eligible? For \"line_search_no_improvement\": yes — subdivision is the remedy (smaller span). For \"trial_state_domain_refused\": also yes (smaller step → admissible). Both bounded by cutbacks_this_step < max_control_cutbacks and min span. When subdivision is exhausted: currently only maximum_iterations_reached gets renamed to minimum_control_step_reached. I'll map both new reasons similarly: if subdivision not possible → keep the classified reason as failure_reason (so stop_reason is specific, e.g. \"line_search_no_improvement\"), status stopped_at_limit. Hmm — the existing code: `if failure_reason == \"maximum_iterations_reached\" and adaptive... : failure_reason = \"minimum_control_step_reached\"`. For the new reasons, when subdivision is exhausted, keep the original classified reason (more informative). Status: stopped_at_limit if steps else diverged — unchanged.\n\nWait, one more consideration: `_nonlinear_status_category(status, failure_reason)` at 3433 — check it doesn't whitelist reasons. Let me look at it later.\n\nNow, about the constraint-satisfied guard for the no-improvement failure: use the same convergence criterion `constraint_error <= tolerance * target_scale`. In the cycle, constraint_rhs = 0.0 → satisfied. At iteration 1, constraint_error = span > tol*target_scale → keep full step. \n\nEdge: what if an iteration has constraint satisfied AND no improving alpha, but the full step would still eventually converge (non-monotone Newton)? Then we now fail the attempt and subdivide — the subdivided retry from the same start with smaller span... wait, subdividing doesn't change the CURRENT iteration's start; the attempt restarts from the last accepted state (attempt start = last accepted). The smaller span changes the target → different Newton step → maybe monotone. This is a behavior change for robustness; bounded; classified. Acceptable per brief.\n\nNow, is there also a \"state defect\" component? The brief mentions \"repair compatible Newton/globalization/state defect\". The exact 3-cycle with residuals repeating to 13 digits — including across line-search trials with alpha=1 — indicates deterministic plastic flip-flop. With the no-improvement classification, cycling is cut short. I think that's the globalization repair.\n\nLet me also double check the interp ValueError location: line search `u_trial = full_u(q_trial)` at 4220 — yes, inside the line search trial loop. With SO3MeanDomainError caught there → continue halvings. \n\nNow implement:\n\n1. corotational.py: add class SO3MeanDomainError(ValueError) with docstring; change the domain-guard raises in _so3_weighted_mean:\n   - input chart: \"interpolated SO(3) mean is outside the principal rotation-vector chart\"\n   - ambiguous two-master geodesic\n   - anchor ball admission (\"requires a unique convex anchor ball of radius pi/4\")\n   - non-convergence\n   - left anchor ball\n   - mean chart boundary\n   - singular B\n   Keep finite/nonneg/partition as ValueError. Hmm — should the input-chart guard (master thetas out of chart) be a domain error? Yes — a trial state can push a master rotation beyond π. That's the exact interp failure (\"interpolated SO(3) mean is outside the principal rotation-vector chart\" — raised by the INPUT check `any(norm(t) >= chart_limit)`). Yes, that's the one from the traceback (line 1248 = the input chart check). So it must be SO3MeanDomainError.\n\n   Where to define: near the top of corotational.py or just above _so3_weighted_mean. Check if corotational.py has an exceptions section... I'll define it right before _so3_weighted_mean. Also export? Tests import from anysolver.corotational — fine.\n\n2. nonlinear_static.py DC loop:\n   - import: `from .corotational import SO3MeanDomainError`? Check what nonlinear_static already imports from corotational. There's `build_nonlinear_rotating_mpc_map` presumably. Add SO3MeanDomainError to that import.\n   - Line search: wrap trial evaluation:\n     ```python\n     trial_admissible = True\n     try:\n         u_trial = full_u(q_trial)\n         f_int_trial, _k, _s = _assemble_nonlinear_system(...)\n         ... loads ...\n         residual_trial = reduce_force(...)\n         constraint_trial = abs(target - float(row_full @ u_trial))\n     except SO3MeanDomainError:\n         trial_admissible = False\n     ```\n     If not admissible → continue to next alpha. If admissible → compute merit_trial; if passes → accept alpha, break.\n     Track `any_admissible_trial` maybe.\n   - After the trial loop: if no alpha passed:\n     - if constraint_error <= tolerance * target_scale (constraint already met): failure_reason = \"line_search_no_improvement\"; break (out of iteration loop → rollback path).\n     - else: keep full step (unchanged).\n     Wait — but if no alpha passed and we keep the full step, and the full-step endpoint is inadmissible (domain error at alpha=1 trial), we'd commit an inadmissible state. But if alpha=1 trial raised SO3MeanDomainError, it was counted inadmissible, not \"passed\". So \"no alpha passed\" includes the case where alpha=1 was inadmissible. Keeping the full step then would commit an inadmissible endpoint! So the rule needs: keep the full step ONLY if the alpha=1 trial was admissible (evaluated). If alpha=1 was inadmissible → classified failure \"trial_state_domain_refused\" regardless of constraint state.\n     \n     Also if constraint unsatisfied AND alpha=1 admissible but non-improving → keep full step (unchanged behavior).\n     If constraint satisfied AND no improving alpha (admissible or not) → \"line_search_no_improvement\" failure... but if the reason is domain refusal, use \"trial_state_domain_refused\". Distinguish: if not any trial admissible → trial_state_domain_refused; elif no improving → line_search_no_improvement (with constraint-satisfied guard) else keep full step.\n     \n     Hmm, subtle: some trials admissible but none improving, constraint unsatisfied → keep full step (alpha=1 was admissible since trials run from alpha=1 first). Good.\n     \n     All trials inadmissible (even alpha=2^-7) → the committed state is admissible; tiny steps from it should be admissible... if all 8 are inadmissible, something's odd, but classify trial_state_domain_refused → rollback+subdivide. Fine.\n   - Commit guard: after the line search block, before contact candidate / commit: if adaptive route and rotating map... actually the commit `q += delta` happens for all routes. The full-step fallback path (no line search run? The line search only runs `if adaptive_control_subdivision and np.any(delta)`). If adaptive_control_subdivision is False, no line search, no trial evaluation — the full step is committed untested; a domain refusal at the endpoint would abort at next loop top. Should I guard there too? The brief's outcome is about the adaptive route; but a commit guard is cheap and uniform. However, for the non-adaptive route there's no subdivision remedy — a domain refusal there would become a classified failure with rollback → status stopped_at_limit/diverged. That's still better than an abort. But careful: \"Preserve strict intrinsic-mean domain\" — refusing is correct; the question is only whether it aborts or classifies. I'll apply the commit guard on the rotating route generally (rotating_mpc_map is not None), setting failure_reason = \"trial_state_domain_refused\" → break. The rollback path handles restore. For the non-adaptive route, no subdivision → classified stop. OK.\n     \n     Implementation of commit guard: \n     ```python\n     if rotating_mpc_map is not None:\n         try:\n             full_u(q + delta[:-1])\n         except SO3MeanDomainError:\n             failure_reason = \"trial_state_domain_refused\"\n             break\n     ```\n     But this doubles full_u calls... only kinematics, cheap. But wait — in the line-search case, the accepted alpha endpoint was already evaluated (full_u(q_trial) succeeded) — the commit guard re-evaluates the same point. Minor waste, acceptable. Actually I can skip the guard when the line search accepted an alpha (endpoint known admissible): only guard when line search didn't run or kept the full step unevaluated... but the full-step fallback case: alpha=1 trial WAS evaluated (it ran first). Hmm: trials run alpha=1 first always (line_reduction=0). So when the line search runs, alpha=1's endpoint was always evaluated. The only unevaluated-commit case: line search didn't run (adaptive off or delta zero). So: guard only when the line search did not evaluate. Simpler: track `endpoint_verified = False`; set True when any trial evaluated (alpha=1 always evaluated when line search runs and merit_current>0). If line search didn't run → guard evaluates. Clean.\n\n     Hmm wait, one more case: merit_current == 0.0 → line search skipped entirely (the `if merit_current > 0.0` condition). Then delta committed untested → guard applies. OK.\n\n   - Subdivision eligibility: extend the two conditions:\n     ```python\n     and failure_reason in (\"maximum_iterations_reached\", \"line_search_no_improvement\", \"trial_state_domain_refused\")\n     ```\n     for the subdivision retry; and the second condition (rename to minimum_control_step_reached) — currently only for maximum_iterations_reached. For the new reasons, when subdivision is exhausted, keep the specific reason (don't rename). Actually for consistency: if failure_reason == \"maximum_iterations_reached\" → rename to minimum_control_step_reached (existing); else keep. Fine.\n\n   - Also: the no-improvement failure must break out of the iteration loop cleanly: `break` inside the iteration for-loop → then `step_iterations += iteration`... careful: `break` from the iteration loop goes to `step_iterations += iteration` — `iteration` holds the current value. Existing failure breaks (e.g. singular_augmented_tangent) use the same pattern. Fine.\n\n   One more check — where exactly is the line search's \"no alpha passed\" state? `line_search_alpha` stays 1.0 if no alpha passed... and also stays 1.0 if alpha=1 passed immediately. Need to distinguish \"alpha=1 passed\" from \"nothing passed\". Currently: `line_search_alpha = 1.0` initial; loop breaks when a trial passes with `line_search_alpha = alpha`. If nothing passes, line_search_alpha remains 1.0 — indistinguishable from alpha=1 passing. I need a flag: `line_search_accepted = False` → set True on pass. Then:\n   ```python\n   if not line_search_accepted:\n       if not any_trial_admissible:\n           failure_reason = \"trial_state_domain_refused\"; break\n       if constraint_error <= tolerance * target_scale:\n           failure_reason = \"line_search_no_improvement\"; break\n       # else keep full step (unchanged)\n   ```\n   And when line_search_accepted and alpha<1 → scale delta (existing).\n\n   Wait, also need `any_trial_admissible` — set True when a trial evaluates without domain error.\n\n   Also the merit pass condition currently `if merit_trial <= (1-1e-4*alpha)*merit_current: line_search_alpha = alpha; break` → add `line_search_accepted = True`.\n\n3. Status category: check `_nonlinear_status_category` handles unknown failure_reasons safely (line 3433). Let me read it.\n\n4. Tests: add focused regression in tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3.py (owned):\n   - Trial-domain refusal: monkeypatch the rotating map's apply_kinematics (or _so3_weighted_mean) to raise SO3MeanDomainError for trials beyond a bound, on a small model; assert: solve does NOT raise; returns classified failure (stop_reason trial_state_domain_refused) or backtracks and completes; committed state preserved (displacements equal last accepted / zero if none); no constitutive mutation (element states unchanged vs deep copy).\n   - Line-search no-improvement classification: harder to force without heavy fixtures... Could monkeypatch _assemble_nonlinear_system to return a residual that never improves? E.g., a fixture where after the constraint is satisfied, every trial residual is larger. Simpler: monkeypatch reduce_force? These are internal. Alternative: use the same small model with a monkeypatched assemble that adds a perturbation making merit increase... The zero-span test pattern (monkeypatch _assemble_nonlinear_system) is established in this module. For no-improvement: make the assembled force such that the residual never decreases — e.g., return force with a growing perturbation each call. Then the attempt should classify line_search_no_improvement (if constraint satisfied) and stop with bounded attempts rather than cycling 40 iterations. Assert: status stopped_at_limit, stop_reason line_search_no_improvement, attempts bounded (control_attempts small), committed state preserved (zero displacement if first step... constraint satisfied requires the control to have been met — with a perturbed force the first iteration satisfies constraint? Hmm.)\n\n   Design the no-improvement test concretely: small eccentric model (like zero-span test uses _build_coupled_cantilever(\"eccentric\", nx=2, ny=1)), displacement control target small (e.g. -0.001), num_steps=1, max_iterations=40. Monkeypatch assemble: after the first call (which lets the first iteration compute a step), return force + perturbation that grows with call count so every trial residual is worse. Actually simpler: make the residual oscillate deterministically like the cycle: force_pert = A*(-1)^call. Then no trial improves reliably... risky, might accidentally improve.\n\n   Alternative cleaner test: monkeypatch `nonlinear_static.factorize` to return a handle whose solve returns a fixed delta that satisfies the row (so constraint becomes satisfied) but never reduces residual? That's heavy-handed but deterministic: delta = [0...0, dlam]? The commit q += delta; next iteration constraint satisfied (row_red@q...). Hmm, if delta's dq part is zero except... the control row must be satisfied: row_jac @ dq = constraint. If I return dq=0, constraint stays unsatisfied → the no-improvement guard (constraint satisfied) won't trigger; full step kept. Not good.\n\n   Let me think about the domain-refusal test — the primary required regression (\"Add focused regression for trial refusal and preserved state\"). Design: small interp model (the slice3 module has _build_coupled_cantilever(\"interpolated\"?) — check what couplings exist). Use displacement control with a target that requires a Newton step; monkeypatch corotational._so3_weighted_mean to raise SO3MeanDomainError whenever the trial state's master rotations differ from the committed one... Hmm, but the mean is called for committed states too (loop top full_u(q)). I need refusal ONLY for trials, not for the committed state. \n\n   Approach: monkeypatch `NonlinearRotatingMPCMap.apply_kinematics`? It's called by full_u for both trials and committed states. Distinguish: trials are evaluated with tangent=False assemblies... no, apply_kinematics is separate from assembly.\n\n   Better: wrap `_so3_weighted_mean` to raise only when a global \"refuse_next_trials\" flag is set, and set that flag by patching `_assemble_nonlinear_system` to flip it on when called with tangent=False (line-search trial) — i.e., refuse any trial AFTER the first line-search trial begins. Sequence: iteration 1: full_u(q) at top (flag off, OK), assemble tangent=True, factorize, solve, line search: full_u(q_trial) → flag was flipped by...? The flag flips when assemble(tangent=False) is called, but full_u(q_trial) happens BEFORE the trial assembly. Order in line search: u_trial = full_u(q_trial) → assemble(tangent=False). So flipping on tangent=False assembly refuses the NEXT trial's full_u. Trial 1 (alpha=1) evaluates fine, then flips flag; trial 2 (alpha=0.5) full_u raises → inadmissible; trials 3-8 raise → all inadmissible... but trial 1 was admissible and maybe passed the merit test → accepted → no refusal exercised. Hmm.\n\n   Alternative: refuse trials based on state: patch apply_kinematics to raise SO3MeanDomainError if the input u's master rotation norm exceeds the committed one by a factor... complicated.\n\n   Simplest robust approach: patch `nonlinear_static._assemble_nonlinear_system` with a wrapper that, when called with tangent=False (line-search trial), raises SO3MeanDomainError? No — the domain error is raised in full_u before assembly.\n\n   OK different approach: patch full_u? It's a closure inside the solver — can't patch.\n\n   Patch `NonlinearRotatingMPCMap.apply_kinematics` with a wrapper that raises SO3MeanDomainError when called with a state whose control value is beyond the current committed control... The wrapper doesn't know the committed control. But it can compare against a captured \"baseline\" u captured at the first call (the committed initial state): refuse if the trial's probe-dof displacement differs from baseline by more than epsilon... but committed states also evolve.\n\n   Hmm — actually, simpler: refuse based on call-order asymmetry: the line search calls apply_kinematics(q_trial) then assemble(tangent=False). The main loop calls full_u(q) then assemble(tangent=True). So: patch assemble to record the LAST u it was called with (committed or trial). Patch apply_kinematics to raise if... no.\n\n   Cleanest: patch `_so3_weighted_mean` (the actual domain failure point) to raise SO3MeanDomainError when the mean's input thetas differ from the last committed thetas by more than a tolerance... still stateful.\n\n   Wait — actually, maybe I'm overcomplicating. The natural unit-level regression: the trial-refusal logic lives in the DC loop. I can test it at the loop level with a REAL small model and a REAL domain violation: choose a displacement-control target so large that the first Newton step's trial endpoint genuinely leaves the chart? For a two-master interp model with big rotations — the full-target interp case does exactly this but costs ~100+s. A smaller version: fewer steps... the chart violation needs master rotations near π — that's the -0.20 territory (large rotation ~30°+... wait π is 180°; master rotations at -0.20 are ~28-30° = 0.5 rad, far from π. Hmm! Then why did the interp full target hit the chart guard?? \"interpolated SO(3) mean is outside the principal rotation-vector chart\" is the INPUT check: any master theta norm >= π-1e-6. At ~30° rotations, that can't trigger... unless the trial overshot catastrophically: the line-search trial q_trial = q + alpha*delta with alpha=1 and a huge delta (the \"first postyield Newton step can overshoot the load factor catastrophically\" scenario) → master rotations way beyond π. Yes! That's consistent: an overshooting Newton step whose trial has insane rotations → chart refusal. So the refusal happens on wild trials, and backtracking to small alpha tames them. \n\n   So for a focused test, I need a trial that overshoots wildly. On a small model with fiber plasticity postyield, the first Newton step can overshoot. That's what the real fixture does at -0.20. A cheaper trigger: monkeypatch factorize's solve to return an inflated delta (e.g., 100x the true solution) for the first solve — then the line-search trial at alpha=1 is wild; if I inflate enough, the trial master rotations exceed π → real SO3MeanDomainError from the real map. Then the line search should backtrack: smaller alphas admissible; if none improves merit → classified failure with preserved state; or some alpha improves → accepted and solve continues (maybe then converges or fails bounded). Either outcome asserts: no exception propagates; committed state preserved (rollback verified by displacements equal the pre-attempt state or valid accepted step); no constitutive mutation on refused trials (element states of refused trials never committed — check model states unchanged from deep copy when failure).\n\n   Inflating delta via factorize wrapper: return handle whose solve returns 100*delta. But then the committed steps also inflate... only the first solve? Make the wrapper inflate only the first solve call. After backtrack/accept, subsequent solves are genuine. The solve might then converge normally to the target. Assertions: result completes OR classified-fails; no ValueError propagation; if failed: displacements equal initial (rollback) and element states equal deep-copied initial; if completed: constraint postcheck passes. Also assert control_line_search_reductions >= 1 or stop_reason in the classified set.\n\n   Hmm, this is getting elaborate but it's a genuine focused regression through the real map. The zero-span test in the module already monkeypatches _assemble_nonlinear_system similarly, so the pattern fits.\n\n   Also a second cheap test: unit-level — SO3MeanDomainError is raised (not plain ValueError... it IS a ValueError subclass) by _so3_weighted_mean for out-of-chart input, and the DC loop imports it. Maybe assert `issubclass(SO3MeanDomainError, ValueError)` and that the chart guard raises it — trivial, keeps the type contract.\n\n   And the no-improvement classification test: monkeypatch assemble to add a large oscillating perturbation to the force AFTER the constraint is satisfied... Let me think: with the eccentric small model, 1 step to a small target. Iteration 1: constraint unsatisfied → full step kept regardless. Iteration 2: constraint satisfied (approximately); if the perturbed force makes every trial residual worse than current → line_search_no_improvement → rollback → subdivision (span halves) → attempt 2 iteration 1: constraint unsatisfied (span smaller) → full step → iteration 2: again no improvement → ... 8 cutbacks → stop with failure_reason line_search_no_improvement, status stopped_at_limit, and displacements rolled back to zero (no accepted step), element states unchanged. Bounded: attempts = 1+8=9, iterations ~2 each. Fast. \n\n   Perturbation design: force_pert = c * (-1)^k? The residual at trial states... The merit test: merit_trial <= (1-1e-4*alpha)*merit_current. If the perturbation alternates sign each assembly call, trial merits alternate high/low... could accidentally pass. Make it strictly increasing: perturbation grows with call count: force += n_calls * A * unit_vector. Then every subsequent trial residual is larger than current → no trial ever improves → no-improvement classification. But careful: this also affects the main-loop assemblies (residual at iteration k+1 larger) — fine, it never converges; each attempt: iter1 constraint-unsatisfied full step; iter2 constraint-satisfied, trials all worse → class