ing half-target runs where constraint is satisfied, no alpha improves, and the full step eventually converged? If yes, the new rule turns them into attempt failures + subdivision — the subdivided retry would probably still converge (smaller span), so the half cases would still pass but with different step records... The per-snapshot assertions check equilibrium/wrench, not iteration counts. Risk: cutback bound (8) exhausted → stop. In the probe trace, the cycle iterations (C→A with 8 trials no improvement) are exactly the ones we want to fail. In the earlier phase (n=397 area), iterations show trials 1-8 with improving acceptance mostly.\n\nI can't fully verify without running; the half-target EP test is ~27s per the earlier evidence (both routes). If my focused pytest budget allows, include the half EP tests as regression: -k \"half\" on the continuation module took 26.77s (slice3-so3-ep-half-1). That's worth it: it directly checks the new rule against the passing acceptance. Budget: focused new tests ~20s + half EP ~30s ≈ 50s, leaving ~50s. Maybe also the zero-span + intrinsic-mean selection (~4s). Then handoff with ~40s unspent. The direct/interp full-target verification left to root (unmet gate, stated).\n\nHmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider the constraint-satisfied guard once more. In the cycle, at state C (residual 76.5, constraint 0): no alpha improves → fail attempt → subdivide. The attempt start = last accepted state (the state where the current milestone attempt began). Subdivision halves the remaining span. Retry from attempt start. The Newton step from attempt start with half span — the first iteration has constraint unsatisfied (span to target) → full-step fallback allowed → ... this reproduces the old pre-line-search behavior at smaller spans, which DID accept increments (steps 11-14 in the old run) after multiple cutbacks. So the direct case will likely still grind forward incrementally, bounded by 8 cutbacks per scheduled step... The old run had 1