{"id": "reasoning-c442d738-fa82-5d85-81ea-6036b3aa5b0f-0", "sessionId": "3290534b-2fa0-0d5b-1a6c-5caaf888f672", "turnId": "turn-efff736df4afd6e2b448c337e3fb4175", "createdAt": 1792352184114, "updatedAt": 1792352184114, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "For state preservation, the simplest robust approach mirroring the zero-span test: assert displacements are zero (rollback to initial) and that the solve returns a classified failure. For constitutive state, I can capture the model's element states before and after via the same mechanism the solver uses... The zero-span test doesn't check states. But the brief says \"no constitutive mutation on refused trials\" — the trial states are discarded via `_discard_nonlinear_state_candidate`. I can verify at the mechanism level: refused trials never get assembled (my B3 `continue` skips assembly) — so no constitutive evaluation happens for refused trials at all. That's stronger: count assembly calls with tangent=False and compare... hmm, can't distinguish refused (no assembly) from admissible trials.\n\nA cleaner state assertion: use `initial_element_states` input route? That's for restart. Alternatively check `result.info[\"strain_summary\"]`? For an elastic model, states are corotational references — probably empty dicts.\n\nPragmatic approach: capture deep copies of the model's nonlinear element states before the solve and after, using the same accessor the solver uses. What is committed_states initially? `_assemble_nonlinear_system(model, u, committed_states, ...)` — committed_states built from model elements' state. Is there a public getter? In the continuation tests they pass `initial_element_states` from `pre.element_states` (a prepared model). For our purpose: after a failed solve with zero accepted steps, the model's element states should be unchanged. The zero-span test asserts displacements only.\n\nI'll assert:\n- no exception (the regression: previously a chart