{"id": "reasoning-a58f46ba-9510-58dd-a0c2-4ac74bb1419c-0", "sessionId": "3290534b-2fa0-0d5b-1a6c-5caaf888f672", "turnId": "turn-efff736df4afd6e2b448c337e3fb4175", "createdAt": 1792352218802, "updatedAt": 1792352218802, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Now append the focused regression tests to tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3.py (LF endings). Also add SO3MeanDomainError to the corotational import.\n\nTests:\n1. test_so3_mean_domain_refusal_is_typed\n2. test_dc_trial_domain_refusal_is_backtracked_not_aborted (interp, inflated factorize)\n3. test_dc_line_search_no_improvement_is_classified_and_rolls_back (eccentric, growing perturbation)\n\nFor test 2, the handle: the DC loop calls `factorize(aug, MatrixClass.GENERAL, signature=...)` then `handle.solve(rhs)`. My wrapper returns an object with .solve. Note: factorize might also be called during model initialization (e.g., equilibration) — with no initial fields, probably not. But `solve_static_nonlinear` may factorize elsewhere (e.g., for the initial load factor recovery?). The wrapper counts calls; if other calls happen they'd also be inflated — could break initialization. With no initial state and force... the DC route: `_solve_static_displacement_control` — any factorize before the loop? The `recover_initial_load_factor` path does a least-squares... only when requested. Default False. OK.\n\nAlso careful: in test 2, the model is \"interpolated\" — the rotating map has 2-master entries; the chart guard triggers on master rotations ≥ π−1e-6. The inflated dq: does dq actually contain master ROTATION dofs? The reduced q includes free master dofs (rotations included). The tip load -100 N uz → bending → master rotations ~0.01 rad. ×1e4 → 100 rad. Definitely ≥ π. But wait — the trial q_trial = q + alpha*delta[:-1] where delta = 1e4 * true. full_u computes u_lin = T@q_trial + u0, then apply_kinematics reads master rotations from u_lin. Master rotation entries of u_lin = 1e4 × true rotation change ~ 100 rad → chart guard → SO3MeanDomainError. \n\nOne catch: `_so3_weighted_mean` first checks finiteness — fine; then len==2 → chart check on inputs → raise. Good.\n\nAlso possible: before the chart check, `master_rotations(u)` reads u[m_theta] — fine.\n\nTest 2 assertions:\n- result returned (no exception).\n- failure_reason == \"trial_state_domain_refused\"\n- status != \"completed\"; steps empty; snapshots == ()\n- displacements zero\n- factorize_calls <= 20 (bounded; expect 9)\n- adaptation cutbacks <= 8\n\nTest 3 assertions:\n- failure_reason == \"line_search_no_improvement\"\n- status != \"completed\"; steps empty; snapshots ()\n- displacements zero\n- assemble_calls <= 200 (expect ~162)\n\nHmm wait — test 3 trace again with the residual-dominated guard: attempt 1, iteration 1: assemble call 1 (pert 1e6). residual = F_ext - F_int where F_int includes +1e6 at tip uz dof... The reduced residual = J^T(F_ext - F_int) ~ -1e6 * (row) → norm ~1e6-ish (reduced). constraint = -0.01. Solve → delta (true). Line search: merit_current = res/(tol*ref) + constraint/(tol*scale). ref: ref_vec = reduce(u, F_const + max(|lam|,1)*F_prop) = reduced proportional load ~100 N → reference = max(100,1)=100. res term: 1e6/(1e-8*100) = 1e12. constraint term: 0.01/(1e-8*0.01)=1e8. Residual dominates already at iteration 1! Trials: call 2 (pert 2e6) → res_trial ~2e6 → merit worse → fail... all 8 fail → no acceptance → guard: residual dominates → line_search_no_improvement at ITERATION 1 → break → rollback → subdivide. Each attempt: 1 iteration, 9 assemble calls (1 + 8 trials) → 9 attempts × 9 = 81 calls. Bounded. failure_reason = line_search_no_improvement. \n\nHmm — but is that the desired product behavior? At iteration 1 the state is the initial one and the constraint is unsatisfied (control move pending), but the residual dominates because of my artificial perturbation. The guard fires. For the test that's fine (deterministic). For the product: a control-move iteration with a huge residual and no improving alpha → classified failure → subdivide → smaller control step → retry. Reasonable robustness behavior.\n\nBut wait — now reconsider the REAL EP run iteration n=1 (fresh solve from zero state): residual 0 → residual term 0 → constraint dominates → full step kept. Good. n=2: res 441098, constraint ~0 → residual dominates; but an alpha improved (accepted) → no classification. Good.\n\nWhat about real iterations where no alpha improves AND residual dominates AND the full step would have led to convergence? E.g., n=397-417 region: the cycle — we WANT classification there. Other spots: from the probe, trials_prev=8 appeared at n=2 (no improvement, constraint ~0 → residual dominates → would NOW classify at n=2!). Wait n=2: 8 trials, no acceptance... but earlier I analyzed n=2→n=3: residual 441098→169.65, and trials_prev at n=3 was 1. Let me recheck: at solve n=2, trials_prev=8 → the line search after solve n=1 ran 8 trials, all failed, full step kept → state at n=2 has residual 441098. At solve n=3, trials_prev=1 → line search after n=2 ran 1 trial (alpha=1, accepted: 441098→169). So the no-improvement event happened after solve n=1 — the step from the INITIAL state (residual 0 at n=1, constraint -1/60). At that point: residual_norm = 0 → residual term 0 → constraint dominates → full step kept under the new guard. Phew — correct, the guard does NOT fire there. \n\nAnd the cycle C-state: res 76.5, constraint 0 → fires. \n\nNow the earlier phase n=393-396 (res 168→163→154→151, trials 3,3,4,6): those had acceptances (improving alphas found). n=397: constraint -6.5e-5 (new subdivided attempt start, converged state res 1e-7): iteration 1 of the new attempt: res 1e-7 → constraint dominates → full step kept → n=398 res 367.96... then n=398: res 368, constraint ~0 → residual dominates; trials at n=399: 1 (accepted, 368→70). n=400: res 70, trials 8 → no improvement after n=400?? At n=401: res 1058 (worse!) — so after solve n=400 (state res 70), 8 trials all failed → full step → 1058. Under the new guard: at state res 70, constraint ~0 → residual dominates → line_search_no_improvement → attempt fails → subdivide. Exactly the intended repair — the cycle is cut at its entry.\n\nNow — the direct full target under the repair: attempts fail fast (2-3 iterations each), subdivide 8 times per scheduled milestone, then stop with line_search_no_improvement (or complete if increments converge). Either way bounded and classified. \n\nAlso verify: does the guard reference `residual_norm` and `reference` in scope at that point? Yes — both defined earlier in the iteration (residual_norm, reference computed before the convergence check; the line-search block is after). Good.\n\nNow write the tests. Also update the module docstring? The module docstring lists R1-R5 coverage; the new tests extend coverage — add a line. Keep minimal: add to docstring \"final repair: typed SO(3) mean domain refusal, DC trial-domain rejection with preserved committed state, and classified line-search no-improvement failure\". \n\nImport addition: SO3MeanDomainError in the corotational import block.\n\nLet me append via python (LF file).", "summary": []}
