{"id": "reasoning-55bc4efe-29b9-568a-8c95-3e7eb40523e0-0", "sessionId": "3290534b-2fa0-0d5b-1a6c-5caaf888f672", "turnId": "turn-efff736df4afd6e2b448c337e3fb4175", "createdAt": 1792352265890, "updatedAt": 1792352265890, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "8 passed (including the trial-domain refusal test and the typed test and zero-span and intrinsic-mean). The no-improvement test failed: failure_reason came back \"minimum_control_step_reached\" instead of \"line_search_no_improvement\".\n\nWhy? Look at the rename block:\n```python\n                    if (\n                        failure_reason == \"maximum_iterations_reached\"\n                        and adaptive_control_subdivision\n                    ):\n                        failure_reason = \"minimum_control_step_reached\"\n```\nThat only renames maximum_iterations_reached. Hmm — so where did minimum_control_step_reached come from? Oh wait — maybe the attempt ended with maximum_iterations_reached (not line_search_no_improvement)! Trace: with the growing perturbation, maybe the line search DID find an improving alpha at some point, so iterations proceeded to max_iterations=5 → maximum_iterations_reached → subdivision → ... eventually min span → renamed minimum_control_step_reached.\n\nWhy would a trial improve? merit_current at iteration 1: res(call1) ~ 1e6·(reduced row) — the perturbation adds 1e6 to F_int at tip uz. Reduced residual = J^T(F_ext − F_int) — the tip uz dof... is the tip a master node? The control node is tip = grid[(nx,0)] — a shell master node. The reduced residual includes −1e6·(that dof's contributions). Trials: call 2 → +2e6 → residual roughly doubles → merit worse. Hmm, but the constraint term: at iteration 1, constraint = 0.01, constraint term = 0.01/(1e-8·0.01) = 1e8. Residual term = res/(1e-8·ref). If res ~ 1e6·c... Actually wait: is the reduced residual really ~1e6? The reduced residual = J^T (F_ext − F_int). J^T maps full → reduced; the tip uz dof is a master dof; J has entries ~O(1) → reduced residual ~ 1e6-ish? Then residual term = 1e6/(1e-8·ref). ref = max(norm(reduced F_prop), 1): F_prop reduced ~ 100 N → ref = 100 → residual term = 1e6/1e-6 = 1e12 >> constraint term 1e8. Trial at alpha=0.5: res(call2) ~ 2e6 → 2e12 > (1−1e-4·0.5)·1e12 → fails. All trials fail → guard: residual-dominated (1e12 vs 1e8) → line_search_no_improvement → break. That's what should happen...\n\nBut the result says the attempts ended with maximum_iterations_reached. So the line search must have ACCEPTED some alpha. How? If residual_trial < residual_current... The perturbation grows per CALL — but wait: the main-loop assembly at iteration k uses call n, then the 8 trials use calls n+1..n+8. The trial residuals grow. Hmm — unless the merit comparison uses `reference` computed from the CURRENT iteration's ref_vec... same for all trials.\n\nOR: the first iteration's line search: merit_current uses residual_norm from call 1 (1e6). Trial 1 (alpha=1): call 2 (2e6) → worse. ... Trial 8 (alpha=2^-7): call 9 (9e6) → worse. No acceptance. Guard: residual-dominated → line_search_no_improvement → break at iteration 1. So failure_reason should be line_search_no_improvement...\n\nUnless — the guard did NOT trigger because residual-dominated check failed: residual_norm*target_scale >= constraint_error*reference → 1e6*0.01 = 1e4 vs constraint_error*reference = 0.01*100 = 1 → 1e4 >= 1 → triggers. Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the issue: `reference` = max(norm(ref_vec),1) where ref_vec = reduce_force(u, F_const + max(|lam|,1)*F_prop). F_const = 0 (all loads proportional? The tip load is the load case → proportional). ref ~ reduced norm of 100 N load ~ maybe ~100·(lever arms) — order 100-600. Still triggers.\n\nAlternatively — maybe the failure came from a DIFFERENT attempt path: after line_search_no_improvement → subdivision → attempt 2 → ... → at some point cutbacks exhausted → the reason stays line_search_no_improvement... but result shows minimum_control_step_reached, which ONLY comes from the rename of maximum_iterations_reached. So some attempt ended with maximum_iterations_reached — meaning its iteration loop ran 5 iterations without the guard firing, and without the line search accepting... wait if no acceptance and guard doesn't fire → full step kept → next iteration → ... → 5 iterations → maximum_iterations_reached.\n\nSo in those attempts the guard didn't fire. Why? Maybe because the line search DID accept alphas (merit improvements) in some iterations: with the growing perturbation... hmm, actually WAIT. I see it: the perturbation grows by CALL COUNT globally. In iteration 2, the main-loop assembly is call 10 (1e7). The trials are calls 11-18 (1.1e7-1.8e7). Trial residual ~ (10+1)e6... vs current 1e7 → worse. Still no improvement.\n\nHmm, unless the residual isn't dominated by the perturbation dof. The perturbation is at the tip uz dof — the CONTROL dof! The control row enforces uz; the augmented solve will compensate the perturbation... The residual = J^T(F_ext − F_int): the −1e6·n at tip uz contributes to the reduced residual through J^T. But hold on — the tip uz is the controlled dof; in the reduced system, is the tip uz dof free? The control row is an equation, not a constraint; the tip uz dof is free in q. So the perturbation enters the reduced residual. OK.\n\nLet me think about merit_trial vs merit_current more carefully with the constraint term: at iteration 2+, constraint_error ≈ 0 (control satisfied). merit ≈ res/(tol·ref). Trials have res growing → worse. Guard: residual-dominated → fires. So iteration 2 should classify...\n\nUnless the line search at iteration 2 accepted alpha because... res_trial with alpha small: q_trial = q + alpha·delta. The perturbation is in F_int at the ASSEMBLY level, independent of state. res_trial = J^T(u_trial)(F_ext − F_int(u_trial) − pert_n). Hmm — J^T depends on u_trial! The reduce_force uses the map's jacobian at u_trial. The perturbation contribution to the reduced residual = −J(u_trial)^T·(pert_n·e_tip). As alpha changes, J changes... but the magnitude ~pert_n regardless. Growing n dominates. So no improvement.\n\nHmm, let me just look at the actual evidence — the test failed with minimum_control_step_reached, meaning at least the flow went through maximum_iterations_reached. Let me debug locally... but budget! Used 2163.3 of 2250.6 → 87.3s left. A quick local debug run (not through harness?) — NO: \"One numerical child at a time through task harness ONLY.\" All numerical children must go through the harness. A debug pytest run costs ~12s through the harness. I have ~87s. \n\nLet me reason more instead of running. Actually — I bet the issue is different: `max_iterations=5`... the attempt ends after 5 iterations with maximum_iterations_reached IF the guard never fires. When does the guard not fire? When `line_search_accepted` is True (an alpha passed) OR residual not dominated OR... \n\nOH WAIT. I see a real bug: in B5, the guard is inside `if adaptive_control_subdivision and np.any(delta):` — fine. But `merit_current > 0.0 and not line_search_accepted` — if the line search accepted, no classification. Fine.\n\nHmm, but what if `np.any(delta)` is False (delta all zeros)? Then no line search, no guard → q += 0 → next iteration → same → 5 iterations → maximum_iterations_reached. Could delta be zero? The augmented solve with the perturbed force — no, delta nonzero.\n\nLet me look again at the possibility that trials DID improve: iteration 1: residual_norm (call 1) = norm(J^T(−1e6·e_tip + F_ext − F_int(0)))... F_int(0) = 0, F_ext = lam·F_prop = 0 (lam=0). So residual = −1e6·J^T e_tip (plus nothing). norm ~ 1e6·||J^T e_tip||. Trial alpha: res_trial = J(u_trial)^T(−(n+1)e6·e_tip + 0·F_prop − F_int(u_trial) + ...) — wait F_ext at trial = F_const + lam_trial·F_prop; lam_trial = alpha·dlam; dlam from the augmented solve ~ small. So res_trial ≈ −(n+1)e6·J^T e_tip + lam_trial·J^T F_prop − J^T F_int(u_trial). The perturbation dominates → res_trial ≈ 2e6·||J^T e_tip||·(1+ε). merit_trial ≈ 2× merit_current·(residual part) → fails Armijo. All trials fail. Guard fires → line_search_no_improvement at iteration 1. So the FIRST attempt should classify...\n\nHmm, BUT: the residual-dominated check: residual_norm·target_scale >= constraint_error·reference. Iteration 1: residual_norm ~ 1e6·||J^T e_tip||. What is ||J^T e_tip||? J = rotating map jacobian + T. The tip uz dof → reduced: T maps... the reduced residual entries ~ O(1)×1e6. Say 1e5-1e6. target_scale = max(|target_increment|, |target_total|, 1e-9) = max(0.01, 0.01, 1e-9) = 0.01. LHS ~ 1e3-1e4. RHS: constraint_error·reference = 0.01·ref. ref = norm(reduced ref_vec) = norm(J^T(F_prop)) ~ 100-ish → RHS ~ 1. LHS >= RHS → fires. \n\nSo why maximum_iterations_reached?! Unless... the guard fired, subdivision happened, and on LATER attempts something changed. After 8 cutbacks the loop stops — with failure_reason = line_search_no_improvement... The rename block:\n```\nif (failure_reason == \"maximum_iterations_reached\" and adaptive_control_subdivision):\n    failure_reason = \"minimum_control_step_reached\"\n```\nDoesn't touch line_search_no_improvement. So the final reason should be line_search_no_improvement.\n\nThe observed minimum_control_step_reached means the LAST attempt ended with maximum_iterations_reached. So in at least the last attempt, 5 iterations ran without classification. Difference between attempts: the perturbation count n is huge by then (call ~100+ → pert ~ 1e8+). Same structure though...\n\nHmm wait — actually, is it possible that in SOME attempt, the line search accepted an alpha, and then convergence... no, it never converges.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe in the first attempt the line search DID accept: Armijo: merit_trial <= (1−1e-4·alpha)·merit_current. Consider alpha=2^-7 with the CONSTRAINT term: at iteration 1, constraint term of merit_current = 0.01/(1e-8·0.01) = 1e8. Residual term ~ 1e6/(1e-8·ref). If ref ~ 100: 1e6/1e-6 = 1e12. merit_current ≈ 1e12. Trial: res ~ 2e6 → 2e12 → fails. Yes fails.\n\nHmm hold on — maybe ref is much LARGER: ref_vec = reduce_force(u, F_const + max(|lam|,1)·F_prop). Wait — the wrapper perturbs F_int (assemble returns force = F_int). But `_weighted_external_load_system` is NOT perturbed. ref_vec uses external loads only → ~100. OK.\n\nSo why?! Let me look at the actual flow again... AH WAIT. I think I see it: `constraint_error` at iteration 1 — the guard uses `residual_norm * target_scale >= constraint_error * reference`. Fine. But the classification `break` — where does it break to? The `break` is inside `if merit_current > 0.0 and not line_search_accepted:` which is inside `if adaptive_control_subdivision and np.any(delta):` — inside the `for iteration` loop. `break` exits the for-iteration loop. Then `step_iterations += iteration`... then `if failure_reason is not None or augmented_contact_manager is None: break` — exits the augmentation for-loop. Then the failure block: discard, restore, subdivision check:\n```\nattempted_control_span = abs(milestone - attempt_start_control) * attempt_fraction\nif (failure_reason in (...) and adaptive and isfinite and >0 and span*0.5 >= min and cutbacks < 8):\n    failure_reason = None; attempt_fraction *= 0.5; ... continue\n```\n`continue` — continues WHAT loop? This is inside the `while`-like control loop over milestones... The `continue` restarts the attempt. OK.\n\nSo each attempt: iteration 1 → classify → subdivide. 8 cutbacks → 9th attempt fails to subdivide → falls through → rename (no) → status = diverged (no steps) → break. failure_reason = line_search_no_improvement. That's what SHOULD happen. But the test got minimum_control_step_reached.\n\nUnless the classification never happened because... `line_search_accepted` was True sometimes? If the line search accepted alpha at iteration 1 of each attempt, then no classification; iterations continue; each iteration accepts some alpha; after 5 iterations → maximum_iterations_reached → subdivide → eventually min span → rename → minimum_control_step_reached. That matches the observation! So trials DID pass the Armijo test sometimes.\n\nWhy would a trial pass? merit_trial <= (1−1e-4·alpha)·merit_current. Hmm — with the growing perturbation, trial residuals grow... UNLESS the trial's lam_trial·F_prop term compensates: res_trial = J^T(F_const + lam_trial·F_prop − F_int_trial − pert_n). The dominant balance: at iteration 1, the augmented solve computed delta to satisfy BOTH residual≈0 and constraint: delta solves [K_red, −F_prop_red; row, 0][dq; dlam] = [residual; constraint]. The solution has dlam such that K_red·dq − F_prop_red·dlam = −residual → with residual dominated by −1e6·J^T e_tip... dlam could be HUGE to compensate the perturbation via F_prop_red! F_prop_red ~ 100 N; to cancel 1e6 residual, dlam ~ 1e4! Then at trial alpha: lam_trial = alpha·1e4; res_trial = J^T(lam_trial·F_prop − F_int(u_trial) − pert_{n+1}·e_tip). The pert_{n+1} = (n+1)·1e6 vs lam_trial·F_prop ~ alpha·1e4·100 = alpha·1e6. So res_trial ≈ J^T(−(n+1)e6·e_tip + alpha·1e6·(F_prop-ish) − ...). These can partially CANCEL! With alpha and n tuned by the solve, res_trial could be smaller than res_current! E.g., alpha=0.5: lam_trial·F_prop contributes +0.5e6·(F_prop direction) vs pert 2e6·e_tip — different vectors, partial cancellation... plausible that some alpha gives merit_trial < merit_current. Then line_search_accepted=True → no classification → proceed. Iteration by iteration the same → 5 iterations → maximum_iterations_reached. And with subdivision, spans shrink but the perturbation keeps growing; eventually min span → minimum_control_step_reached. Matches!\n\nSo my \"never improving\" perturbation isn't never-improving because the load-factor channel compensates. Fix the test: make the perturbation grow MUCH faster than any compensating lam: e.g., pert = 1e12·n? Then dlam to cancel would be ~1e10 — the augmented solve gives whatever dlam; trials: lam_trial·F_prop ~ alpha·dlam·100. dlam ~ pert/F_prop_red ~ 1e12/100 = 1e10 → alpha·1e12·... hmm the cancellation is structural: the augmented solve ALWAYS produces a step that (linearly) zeroes the current residual: K·dq − F_prop·dlam = −res_current. So the alpha=1 trial's NEW residual = O(nonlinearity + pert growth): res_trial ≈ J^T(−Δpert·e_tip) + O(nonlinear) where Δpert = (n+1)−n = 1e6 (the growth per call), NOT the full pert! Because the linear part of the perturbation is cancelled by the Newton step!\n\nSo res_trial ≈ −1e6·J^T e_tip (the increment) + nonlinear terms. res_current ≈ −n·1e6·J^T e_tip... wait no — res_current at iteration k was assembled with pert_n; the Newton step cancels it linearly; the trial at alpha=1 has pert_{n+1} = pert_n + 1e6 → the trial residual ≈ −1e6·J^T e_tip (just the growth) + nonlinearity. So res_trial << res_current when n is large! The Armijo test compares trial vs current: trial ~1e6 vs current ~n·1e6 → trial PASSES easily. That's why iterations proceed — Newton is \"converging\" the perturbed problem, and each iteration the residual drops... but never converges because the perturbation keeps growing by 1e6 each call. After 5 iterations: res ~ 5e6-ish? Actually res_current at iteration k = (n_k − n_{k−1})·1e6·... hmm roughly the last growth increment. It decreases relative... whatever — the mechanism is clear: a GROWING perturbation is partially tracked by Newton through the load factor. My test design was flawed.\n\nBetter design: make the perturbation OSCILLATE in sign with constant magnitude... then trials could alternate improve/worsen; the line search would accept improving ones → no classification → max iterations. Hmm.\n\nWhat perturbation guarantees NO trial improves? The trial residual = J^T(F_ext_trial − F_int_trial − pert). Newton cancels the linearized current residual. The trial residual ≈ J^T(−Δpert) + O(nonlinear + tangent inconsistency). To make EVERY trial worse than current: need the trial residual norm > current residual norm for all alphas. \n\nAlternative: make the perturbation depend on the STATE u rather than call count — e.g., pert = A·(uz_tip − uz_current)? Too clever.\n\nSimplest robust approach: monkeypatch at the RESIDUAL level instead: patch `rotating_mpc_map.reduce_force`? The map is built inside the solver... but I can patch the CLASS method `NonlinearRotatingMPCMap.reduce_force` via monkeypatch.setattr(anysolver.corotational.NonlinearRotatingMPCMap, \"reduce_force\", ...) — a wrapper that returns a FIXED vector with norm slightly varying... Let me think: if reduce_force always returns a fixed vector v (nonzero), then residual_norm is constant; trials: merit_trial = same as merit_current → Armijo requires STRICT decrease (<= (1−1e-4·alpha)·merit) → fixed vector fails → no acceptance → guard: residual-dominated? constraint term at iteration 1: constraint 0.01/(1e-8·0.01)=1e8; residual term = |v|/(1e-8·ref). Choose |v| large, e.g. 1e6 → 1e12 → dominated → classification at iteration 1. Deterministic! And the constraint: the control row is separate (row_full@u) — unaffected by reduce_force patch. \n\nBut patching reduce_force affects the main residual too — the solve never converges (residual fixed at |v|) → good. Every attempt: iteration 1: residual |v|; constraint unsatisfied; line search: trials all have merit_trial == merit_current (same fixed v, constraint_trial: hmm — constraint_trial = |target − row_full@u_trial| varies with alpha! At alpha=1, constraint_trial ≈ 0 → constraint term ~0 → merit_trial = |v|/(tol·ref) vs merit_current = |v|/(tol·ref) + 1e8 → merit_trial < merit_current → PASSES! Oops — at iteration 1 the constraint term dominates merit_current, so the alpha=1 trial (which satisfies the control) massively improves merit → accepted → full step → iteration 2: constraint ≈ 0; merit_current = |v|/(tol·ref); trials: same fixed v, constraint_trial ≈ 0 → merit_trial ≈ merit_current → fails Armijo (needs strict decrease) → no acceptance → residual-dominated (|v|·0.01 vs 0·ref → yes) → line_search_no_improvement → classified at iteration 2. \n\nSo with a fixed-vector reduce_force patch: attempt = 2 iterations, deterministic classification. \n\nBut is patching reduce_force \"fair\"? It simulates an irreducible residual (like the plastic flip-flop cycle where the residual cannot be reduced). The zero-span test patches _assemble_nonlinear_system similarly. Patching the class method affects all rotating maps in that process — fine within the test.\n\nHmm, but reduce_force signature: reduce_force(u, force) → reduced vector. The main loop also uses reduce_force for ref_vec: ref_vec = reduce_force(u, F_const + max(|lam|,1)·F_prop) — patched → ref_vec = fixed v → reference = |v|. Then residual term = |v|/(tol·|v|) = 1/tol = 1e8. And row_jac = row_full @ J(u) — separate. OK.\n\nAlso `_assert` — the wrench/postcheck: on failure no postcheck issues.\n\nLet me compute the flow precisely with |v| = 1e6, tol=1e-8, target_scale=0.01, ref=|v|=1e6:\n- Iteration 1: residual_norm = 1e6. constraint_error = 0.01. reference = max(1e6,1)=1e6. Convergence: 1e6 <= 1e-8·1e6=0.01? No. Solve → delta (nonzero; the augmented system: K_red from the REAL tangent (assemble unpatched), F_prop_red = reduce_force(u, F_prop) = v (patched!) — hmm, F_prop_red = v → the augmented load column is v — the solve gives some delta; fine, nonzero.\n- Line search: merit_current = 1e6/(1e-8·1e6) + 0.01/(1e-8·0.01) = 1e8 + 1e8 = 2e8. Trial alpha=1: u_trial = full_u(q+delta); assemble (real); residual_trial = reduce_force(u_trial, ...) = v (patched, fixed) → residual term 1e8; constraint_trial = |target − row_full@u_trial| ≈ 0 (linear solve enforces row_jac·dq = constraint... but row_jac = row_full@J(u) — real) → constraint_trial small ~O(nonlinear) → merit_trial ≈ 1e8 + small < (1−1e-4)·2e8 → ACCEPTED at alpha=1. line_search_accepted=True. Commit.\n- Iteration 2: residual_norm = 1e6 (fixed v). constraint_error ≈ tiny (say 1e-6). Convergence: 1e6 <= 0.01? No. Sol