{"id": "reasoning-8bc86897-f5f3-500a-80f8-50449da3d6cb-0", "sessionId": "3290534b-2fa0-0d5b-1a6c-5caaf888f672", "turnId": "turn-efff736df4afd6e2b448c337e3fb4175", "createdAt": 1792352268863, "updatedAt": 1792352268863, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Wait — one flaw in the fixed-residual design: the first reduce_force call. Where does it happen and with what? If the first call happens during admission with a zero force, `real_reduce` returns zeros → fixed[\"v\"] = full_like(zeros, 1e6) → shape correct, value 1e6. Good — I use full_like regardless of the real value. Shape comes from the first call — the reduced dimension is constant. Good.\n\nBut another subtlety: `reduce_force` is also called for `F_prop_red = rotating_mpc_map.reduce_force(u, F_prop_current)` — the augmented load column becomes v (all 1e6). The augmented solve: [K_red, −v; row_jac, 0][dq; dlam] = [v; constraint]. The solve is fine (K_red invertible). dlam will be some value. OK.\n\nAnd `ref_vec = reduce_force(u, F_const + max(|lam|,1)·F_prop)` = v → reference = 1e6·sqrt(n_red). Consistent with my analysis (residual term = 1e6·sqrt(n)/1e-8/1e6·sqrt(n) = 1e8). Good.\n\nAlso: does the eccentric route's map get built with entries? Yes.\n\nOne more: `transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters` or postcheck might call reduce_force — on failure path, `constraint_postcheck` uses to_linear_displacement + constraint_residual_summary — not reduce_force. OK.\n\nNow rerun the focused selection. Budget ~87s remaining. Run with timeout 60.", "summary": []}
